{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Nr1CU1JsDKjfxrF1PnPBUY","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (43.6KB). Full output saved to: /Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-10-review-scope/4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4/tool-results/bph59j63h.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n# Area 04: Review scope\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThe scope area decides exactly what a review looks at. Given the Git\ntoplevel and the user's scoping choices (`--staged`, `--base`,\n`--uncommitted-only`, `--paths`, `--exclude`, `--include-vendored`,\n`--scope`), it runs `git` and produces a **Scope**: the diff text the\n**finder** reads (or, for repo scope, a small manifest), the sorted list of\nfiles in scope, the resolved base, the commit/tree identities the rest of\nthe review pins its reads to, and human-facing notes that explain every\nscoping decision.\n\nTwo invariants drive the design:\n\n1. The file list is derived from the same pathspec-filtered git queries\n   that produced the diff text. A file outside the post-exclude diff can\n   never reach the finder, the **verifier**, or the report.\n2. Nothing a repository or the user's Git config can do may run a program\n   on, rewrite, or widen the reviewed content: no external diff drivers,\n   no textconv, no global/system attribute files narrowing scope, no\n   following symlinks out of the tree, and secret-looking untracked files\n   are never sent.\n\nDownstream consumers: the CLI `review` command (prints notes, decides\n\"no diff\", records scope into the **run record**), the context area\n(grounding and file readers pin to `staged` / `stagedTreeOID` /\n`headOID`), and the finder prompt (diff or manifest). The pre-commit\n**gate** reaches this area through `review --staged`.\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\nAll byte counts are UTF-8 byte counts. All git commands below are run as `git\n-C <repo> <args...>` with the parent environment inherited (so an ambient\n`GIT_INDEX_FILE`, `GIT_DIR`, etc. is honored, see 2.6), stdout captured as a\n`Buffer`, and stderr captured for error messages. `<repo>` is always the Git\ntoplevel (from `gitRoot`, 2.13).\n\n### 2.1 Constants\n\n`DEFAULT_IGNORES` (order matters for argument order):\n\n```\nvendor/**\nnode_modules/**\n**/node_modules/**\n*.lock\npackage-lock.json\nyarn.lock\npnpm-lock.yaml\ngo.sum\n*.pb.go\n*_pb2.py\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4","uuid":"bb445b0d-067d-43d6-af11-efdfdc3e0470","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:23:56.629Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Area 04: Review scope\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThe scope area decides exactly what a review looks at. Given the Git\ntoplevel and the user's scoping choices (`--staged`, `--base`,\n`--uncommitted-only`, `--paths`, `--exclude`, `--include-vendored`,\n`--scope`), it runs `git` and produces a **Scope**: the diff text the\n**finder** reads (or, for repo scope, a small manifest), the sorted list of\nfiles in scope, the resolved base, the commit/tree identities the rest of\nthe review pins its reads to, and human-facing notes that explain every\nscoping decision.\n\nTwo invariants drive the design:\n\n1. The file list is derived from the same pathspec-filtered git queries\n   that produced the diff text. A file outside the post-exclude diff can\n   never reach the finder, the **verifier**, or the report.\n2. Nothing a repository or the user's Git config can do may run a program\n   on, rewrite, or widen the reviewed content: no external diff drivers,\n   no textconv, no global/system attribute files narrowing scope, no\n   following symlinks out of the tree, and secret-looking untracked files\n   are never sent.\n\nDownstream consumers: the CLI `review` command (prints notes, decides\n\"no diff\", records scope into the **run record**), the context area\n(grounding and file readers pin to `staged` / `stagedTreeOID` /\n`headOID`), and the finder prompt (diff or manifest). The pre-commit\n**gate** reaches this area through `review --staged`.\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\nAll byte counts are UTF-8 byte counts. All git commands below are run as `git\n-C <repo> <args...>` with the parent environment inherited (so an ambient\n`GIT_INDEX_FILE`, `GIT_DIR`, etc. is honored, see 2.6), stdout captured as a\n`Buffer`, and stderr captured for error messages. `<repo>` is always the Git\ntoplevel (from `gitRoot`, 2.13).\n\n### 2.1 Constants\n\n`DEFAULT_IGNORES` (order matters for argument order):\n\n```\nvendor/**\nnode_modules/**\n**/node_modules/**\n*.lock\npackage-lock.json\nyarn.lock\npnpm-lock.yaml\ngo.sum\n*.pb.go\n*_pb2.py\n*_pb2_grpc.py\n*.min.js\n*.min.css\n```\n\n`PATCH_FLAGS` go on every git invocation that produces a patch or a name\nlist from `diff` (so a repo's `.gitattributes` diff driver, the user's\n`diff.external`, color, and prefix settings cannot change the text):\n\n```\n--no-ext-diff --no-textconv --no-color --src-prefix=a/ --dst-prefix=b/\n```\n\n`DIFF_FLAGS` = `--no-renames` followed by `PATCH_FLAGS`. Every diff-mode\n`git diff` uses `DIFF_FLAGS`. Renames are recovered later (2.8) only for\npairs whose both sides survived the excludes.\n\n`DIFF_CONFIG` = `-c diff.orderFile= -c core.quotePath=false`, placed\nbefore the subcommand. Every\ngit invocation whose subcommand is `diff` in this file, and in the context\narea's own `git diff` calls, runs as `git -C <repo> DIFF_CONFIG diff ...`\n(the argument lists below omit it for brevity). It overrides the user's\n`diff.orderFile` so section order is always git's default path order\n(deliberate change 17, spec.md). The empty value must be verified against\nthe installed git during implementation (a test with a global\n`diff.orderFile` that reverses the order, 5). If git rejects an empty\nvalue or still applies the file, use `-c diff.orderFile=/dev/null`\ninstead; the contract is only that the user's order file has no effect.\n`core.quotePath=false` keeps non-ASCII paths unquoted in patch headers\n(`diff --git a/... b/...`), so the fit breakdown attributes their bytes\nto the right file (deliberate change 15); git still quotes paths with\ntabs, newlines, or double quotes, and those report 0 bytes in the\nbreakdown as in Go.\n\n`BASE_CANDIDATES`, tried in order when no `--base` is given (diff scope\nonly): `origin/main`, `origin/master`, `main`, `master`, `HEAD~1`.\n\n`EMPTY_TREE` = `4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904` (git's empty\ntree; lets a repo with no commits be diffed).\n\n`DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES` = `1_000_000`.\n\n`SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(n)` is exactly this single line (with `n` in\ndecimal, ending in a newline; it exceeds 80 columns by necessity):\n\n```\n\\ roast: the rest of this file's diff is omitted (over <n> bytes); use read_file to inspect the file\n```\n\nThe leading `\\` makes it diff metadata (like git's `\\ No newline at end\nof file`), so the grounder and rename parser skip it as content.\n\n`LINGUIST_ATTRS` = `linguist-generated`, `linguist-vendored`.\n\n### 2.2 Output: the Scope value\n\n- `diff` -- combined diff text: `diffBytes` decoded once as UTF-8 with\n  U+FFFD replacement, for models and reports. Empty in repo scope.\n- `files` -- sorted (bytewise, 2.15) unique repo-relative paths in scope.\n- `base` -- resolved base ref *name* (e.g. `origin/main`), not an OID.\n  Empty for uncommitted-only, staged, no-usable-base, and repo scope.\n- `diffBytes` -- the same combined diff as a `Buffer`: the exact bytes git\n  and the untracked reader produced (after capping), so anything hashed or\n  recorded is byte-exact (cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\", spec.md).\n- `notes` -- human-facing scoping notes, in the order generated. The CLI\n  prints each to stderr.\n- `staged` -- true only for `stagedOnly`; file context must come from the\n  index (or the pinned tree), not the worktree.\n- `stagedTreeOID` -- echo of `BuildOpts.stagedTreeOID` in staged mode;\n  else empty.\n- `manifest` -- survey map (repo scope); empty in diff scope.\n- `survey` -- true for `repo` scope.\n- `headOID` -- repo scope: `rev-parse HEAD`; empty if no commits.\n  Empty in diff scope.\n\n`isEmpty(scope)`: in repo scope, true when `files` is empty; otherwise\ntrue when `diff` is empty after trimming whitespace. The CLI then writes\na report with assessment `NO_DIFF` and exits 0 (CLI area).\n\n### 2.3 Inputs: BuildOpts and how the CLI fills them\n\n- `base` -- `--base` (string, default `\"\"`).\n- `uncommittedOnly` -- `--uncommitted-only`.\n- `stagedOnly` -- `--staged`.\n- `paths` -- `--paths` (string slice: comma-separated and repeatable).\n- `excludes` -- `.roast.json` `review.excludes`, followed by `--exclude`\n  values.\n- `includeVendored` -- `--include-vendored`.\n- `scope` -- `--scope` (`diff` default; the CLI accepts only `diff` or\n  `repo`).\n- `stagedTreeOID` -- with `--staged`, the OID from the context area's\n  `pinIndexTree(repo)`, computed before `build`.\n- `maxFileDiffBytes` -- the whole-diff budget: `limits.max_diff_bytes`,\n  else 1_000_000, or 200_000 with `--quick`.\n\n`maxFileDiffBytes <= 0` (or absent) means `DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES`.\nBelow this, `maxSection` names the effective value.\n\nCLI-level validation (owned by the CLI area, restated for context):\n`--scope` other than `diff`/`repo` is a usage error; `--scope repo` with\n`--quick` is a usage error; `--scope repo` with any of `--staged`,\n`--uncommitted-only`, `--base` is a usage error. `build` itself accepts\n`\"\"`, `diff`, `repo` and rejects anything else with\n`unsupported scope \"<x>\": expected \"\", \"diff\", or \"repo\"`.\n`--staged` together with `--uncommitted-only` or `--base` is not rejected;\nstaged mode simply ignores both.\n\n### 2.4 Common prelude (all scopes)\n\n1. `allExcludes` = `opts.excludes`, then (unless `includeVendored`)\n   `DEFAULT_IGNORES` appended.\n2. Unless `includeVendored`: `linguistExcludes = linguistIgnores(repo,\n   cached = stagedOnly, treeOID = stagedTreeOID)` (2.7). If non-empty,\n   note `excluding <N> path(s) marked linguist-generated/-vendored in\n   .gitattributes (--include-vendored to review)`.\n3. `spec = pathspecArgs(paths, allExcludes, linguistExcludes)`:\n   - if all three lists are empty: no arguments at all;\n   - else `--`, then each path verbatim (git pathspec, user magic allowed),\n     then `:(exclude)<pattern>` per `allExcludes` entry, then\n     `:(exclude,literal)<path>` per `linguistExcludes` entry.\n4. Dispatch on `scope`: `repo` -> 2.12, `\"\"`/`diff` -> 2.5, anything else\n   -> the `unsupported scope` error.\n\nThe linguist step runs before dispatch, so it also runs (uncached) for\nrepo scope.\n\n### 2.5 Diff scope\n\n**No-commit detection.** `head = \"HEAD\"`. Run `rev-parse --verify --quiet\nHEAD` (stdout/stderr discarded). On non-zero exit: `head = EMPTY_TREE`,\nforce `uncommittedOnly = true`, note `repository has no commits;\nreviewing staged + untracked files`.\n\n**Staged mode** (`stagedOnly`), what a commit would contain:\n\n1. `stagedRef` = `[\"--cached\", head]`, or `[head, stagedTreeOID]` when\n   `stagedTreeOID` is set (the pinned tree replaces the live index; same\n   comparison, immutable OID).\n2. Patch: `diff DIFF_FLAGS stagedRef spec` through the section capper\n   (2.9). Failure -> error `git diff (staged): <cause>`.\n3. `notes = truncationNote(notes, cut, maxSection)`.\n4. `diff, n = compactRenames(stagedRef, spec, diff)`; `renameNote(n)`.\n5. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z stagedRef spec`. Failure ->\n   error `git diff --name-only (staged): <cause>`. `files` = the decoded\n   paths (NUL-split, 2.15), sorted.\n6. Return `{diff, files, notes, staged:true, stagedTreeOID}`; `base`\n   empty. No untracked files, no worktree content.\n\n**Default mode** (committed-since-base + uncommitted + untracked):\n\n1. Unless `uncommittedOnly`:\n   1. `resolvedBase = resolveBase(base)`: candidates are `[base]` if given,\n      else `BASE_CANDIDATES`; the first for which `rev-parse --verify\n      --quiet <ref>` exits 0 wins; none -> `\"\"`.\n   2. If `\"\"`: note `no usable base ref (tried <T>); reviewing uncommitted\n      changes only` where `<T>` is the requested base, or\n      `BASE_CANDIDATES.join(\", \")`. Skip to step 2.\n   3. If no base was requested and `resolvedBase !== \"origin/main\"`: note\n      `using base '<resolvedBase>'`.\n   4. `rangeArgs = buildRangeArgs(resolvedBase)`: run `merge-base <base>\n      HEAD` (output discarded); exit 0 -> `[\"<base>...HEAD\"]`, else\n      `[\"<base>\", \"HEAD\"]`.\n   5. Patch: `diff DIFF_FLAGS rangeArgs spec` through the capper. Failure ->\n      `git diff (committed): <cause>`. Cut names go to `cutSections`.\n   6. `committed, n = compactRenames(rangeArgs, spec, committed)`;\n      `renameNote(n)`.\n   7. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z rangeArgs spec`. Failure ->\n      `git diff --name-only (committed): <cause>`.\n2. Uncommitted (worktree and index vs `head`): patch `diff DIFF_FLAGS\n   <head> spec` through the capper; failure -> `git diff (uncommitted):\n   <cause>`; cut names appended to `cutSections`; `compactRenames([head],\n   spec, ...)`; `renameNote`. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z\n   <head> spec`; failure -> `git diff --name-only (uncommitted): <cause>`.\n3. `diff = committed`; if `uncommitted.trim() !== \"\"`, append\n   `\"\\n\\n### Uncommitted changes ###\\n\" + uncommitted` (the separator is\n   added even when `committed` is empty).\n4. `fileSet` = the decoded paths of both name lists (2.15).\n5. Untracked files (2.10) are appended to `diff` and `fileSet`.\n6. `notes = truncationNote(notes, cutSections, maxSection)` -- one note for\n   committed, uncommitted, and untracked cuts together, emitted after the\n   untracked denylist note.\n7. Return `{diff, files: sorted fileSet, base: resolvedBase, notes}`.\n\nName lists use `-z` (deliberate change 15): paths arrive NUL-separated and\nverbatim, never C-quoted, so a path with non-ASCII, tab, or quote characters\nis a normal entry in `files`. Patch text still C-quotes such paths in its\n`diff --git` headers (see 2.8, 2.16). Binary files appear as git prints them\nwithout `--binary` or `--text` (`Binary files a/x and b/x differ`); there is\nno other binary handling for tracked files.\n\n### 2.6 Ambient Git environment\n\nGit subprocesses inherit the full parent environment. In particular a hook\ninvoked by `git commit <paths>` sets `GIT_INDEX_FILE` to an alternate\nindex; staged mode must review that index, not `.git/index`. (The pinned\ntree from `pinIndexTree` already honors it; the `--cached` fallback does\ntoo by inheritance.)\n\n### 2.7 Linguist generated/vendored exclusion\n\nA repo's in-tree `.gitattributes` may mark paths `linguist-generated` or\n`linguist-vendored`; those are treated like `DEFAULT_IGNORES` and are\nreviewed only with `--include-vendored`.\n\n**`linguistIgnores(repo, cached, treeOID)`**, best-effort (every failure\nreturns no excludes, so the review proceeds unfiltered -- never narrower):\n\n1. Pinned index: only when `cached && treeOID`: create a temp dir\n   (`<tmpdir>/roast-scope-idx-*`), `idx = <dir>/index`, extra env\n   `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<idx>`, run `read-tree <treeOID>` with that env.\n   Failure -> remove the dir, return no excludes (do NOT fall back to the\n   live index). The temp dir is removed when the function returns.\n2. `ls-files -z` (with the extra env) -> NUL-split paths, decoded per 2.15\n   (invalid names skipped, counted). Failure -> none.\n3. Only when `cached`: also `diff DIFF_FLAGS --cached --name-only -z`\n   (with the extra env; `DIFF_FLAGS` includes `--no-renames`, deliberate\n   change 16); on success append its paths. With renames off, a staged\n   rename lists both its old and new path, so a staged deletion or rename\n   source of a marked file, absent from the index, is still excluded.\n   Failure here is ignored.\n4. No paths -> none. Else `linguistMarked(paths, cached, env)`, sorted.\n\n**`linguistMarked(repo, paths, cached, env)`**:\n\n- Empty `paths` -> none.\n- Command: `git -C <repo> -c core.attributesFile=/dev/null check-attr\n  --stdin -z [--cached] linguist-generated linguist-vendored`.\n- Environment: parent env, plus `GIT_ATTR_NOSYSTEM=1`, plus `env` last\n  (so a pinned `GIT_INDEX_FILE` wins). These two drop the user-global and\n  system attribute files; only in-tree/index attributes (and\n  `$GIT_DIR/info/attributes`, which cannot be disabled) can narrow scope.\n- Stdin: `paths.join(\"\\0\") + \"\\0\"`.\n- Any failure -> none.\n- Output is NUL-separated `(path, attr, value)` triples; a short trailing\n  group is ignored. A path is marked when `value` is `set` or `true`\n  (`unspecified`, `unset`, `false` do not count, so\n  `dir/x linguist-generated=false` un-marks). Deduplicate, keeping first\n  occurrence order.\n\n**Attribute source per mode**: staged -> index (`--cached`), pinned to\n`stagedTreeOID` when set; every other mode -> worktree `.gitattributes`.\nUntracked candidates always use the worktree (`cached = false`, no pinned\nenv), regardless of mode.\n\nLinguist excludes are exact paths and go into pathspecs with\n`:(exclude,literal)` magic, so a file literally named `*.go` excludes only\nitself.\n\n### 2.8 Rename compaction\n\nDiffs use `--no-renames` so excludes apply to both sides of a move.\n`compactRenames(repo, ref, spec, diffText, maxSection)` then collapses\nmoves whose two sides both survived the excludes. It returns\n`(diffText', pairsCollapsed)` and on any git error returns the input\nunchanged with 0.\n\n**Sections.** `splitSections(text)`: starting at offset 0, each section\nruns up to and including the `\\n` before the next occurrence of\n`\"\\ndiff --git \"` searched from `start + 1`; the last runs to the end.\nLeading text before the first header is its own section.\n`classify(section)`:\n\n- Header = first line. It must start with `diff --git `; the remainder\n  `pair` must not start with `\"`, must have `len >= 7` and\n  `(len - 5)` even; with `n = (len - 5) / 2`, `path = pair[2 .. 2+n)`,\n  require `pair[0..2) == \"a/\"` and `pair[2+n..) == \" b/\" + path`.\n  Otherwise kind `other`.\n- Scan the following lines: the first of `@@...` or `--- ...` -> `other`;\n  `deleted file mode ...` -> `deleted`; `new file mode ...` -> `added`.\n\n**Algorithm.**\n\n1. Collect `deleted` and `added` paths. Either empty -> unchanged, 0.\n2. `diff -M PATCH_FLAGS --name-status -z <ref...> <spec...>` (failure ->\n   unchanged). Walk NUL fields as records: a status field starting with\n   `R` or `C` takes two paths (`from`, `to`), any other status one path\n   (advance 2). A truncated R/C record stops the walk. Accept `(from, to)`\n   only when status starts with `R`, `from` is a deleted path and `to` is\n   an added path of this diff. (Walking whole records is required: a path\n   like `README.md` must never be read as a status.)\n3. No accepted pairs -> unchanged.\n4. `diff -M PATCH_FLAGS <ref...> <spec...>` through the section capper with\n   the same `maxSection` (its cut list is discarded; failure ->\n   unchanged).\n5. For each section of that output, `renameOf(section)`: scan lines until\n   one starts with `@@`; take values of `rename from ` and `rename to `\n   lines; reject when either is empty or starts with `\"` (quoted paths are\n   left uncollapsed). Keep the section when `(from, to)` is accepted; mark\n   both paths `paired`; append the section text, plus `\\n` if it does not\n   end in one.\n6. No paired paths -> unchanged.\n7. Output: every original section in order except `deleted`/`added`\n   sections whose path is paired; then a `\\n` if the output is non-empty\n   and does not end in one; then all kept rename sections. Count =\n   `paired.size / 2`.\n\n`renameNote(n)` for `n > 0`: `collapsed <n> moved file(s) to git's rename\nform (both paths in scope)`. A pure move therefore has a rename header and\nno hunks; a move with a small edit shows only the edited lines.\n\n### 2.9 Patch production and per-file cap\n\n`gitPatch(repo, args, maxSection)` runs git and streams stdout through a\nsection capper, so memory per file is bounded by `maxSection` (a 24 MB\nfile must not be buffered whole). Algorithm on the byte stream, processed\nline by line (a line longer than the read buffer arrives in fragments;\nonly a fragment at line start can be a header):\n\n- State: `header = \"\"`, `used = 0`, `dropping = false`.\n- A fragment at line start beginning with `diff --git ` first finishes the\n  previous section, then sets `header` to that line (without newline),\n  `used = 0`, `dropping = false`.\n- While not dropping, keep `take = min(len, maxSection - used)` bytes of\n  the fragment; if `take < len`, start dropping.\n- Finishing a section that was dropping: if the output does not end in\n  `\\n`, add one; append `SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(maxSection)`; push\n  `sectionName(header)` to `cut`.\n- At EOF, finish the last section.\n- Text before the first header counts toward a section named `\"\"`.\n- Non-zero git exit -> error `exit status <code>: <trimmed stderr>`; a\n  read error kills git and reports the read error.\n\n`sectionName(header)`: strip `diff --git `; if it is `a/P b/P` (same\nlength rule as `classify`), return `P`; else the raw remainder.\n\n`truncationNote(cut, maxSection)` for non-empty `cut`: `diff for <N>\nfile(s) cut at <maxSection> bytes each: <listSome(cut, 10)>`.\n`listSome(items, 10)`: up to 10 joined by `, `; beyond 10, the first 10\nthen `, and <K> more`.\n\nThe cut is at a byte offset and may split a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence;\nsee 3.4 for decoding.\n\n### 2.10 Untracked files (default diff mode only)\n\n1. `ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z` (deliberate change 15);\n   failure -> error `git ls-files --others: <cause>`.\n2. For each decoded path `u` (2.15; names that are not valid UTF-8 are\n   skipped and counted), in git's order:\n   - skip unless `untrackedPasses(u, paths, allExcludes)` (2.14);\n   - if `snapshot.denylisted(u)` -> add to `denied` (the snapshot area's\n     secret denylist; applies even with `--include-vendored`);\n   - else candidate.\n3. `denied` non-empty -> note `skipped <N> untracked file(s) matching the\n   secret denylist: <listSome(denied, 10)>`.\n4. Unless `includeVendored`, drop candidates returned by\n   `linguistMarked(repo, candidates, cached=false, env=none)`.\n5. For each remaining candidate, `synthesizeNewFileDiff(u, maxSection)`;\n   empty result -> skipped silently (not added to `files`); cut -> add `u`\n   to `cutSections`; else add the block and add `u` to `fileSet`.\n6. If any blocks: if `diff.trim() !== \"\"` append `\"\\n\\n### Untracked files\n   (treated as new-file additions) ###\\n\"`; then append\n   `blocks.join(\"\\n\")`.\n\n**`synthesizeNewFileDiff(relpath, maxSection)`**:\n\n- `lstat(<repo>/<relpath>)`; not a regular file (symlink, dir, missing,\n  error) -> `\"\"`. Symlinks are never followed.\n- Read at most `maxSection + 1` bytes; any error -> `\"\"`. `cut =\n  bytesRead > maxSection`.\n- Split on `\\n`; drop one trailing empty element.\n- `header = \"diff --git a/P b/P\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++\n  b/P\\n\"` with `P = relpath` verbatim (no quoting).\n- For each line: if `len(header) + len(body) + len(line) + 2 >\n  maxSection`, set `cut` and stop; else append `\"+\" + line + \"\\n\"` and\n  count it.\n- Result: header, `@@ -0,0 +1,<count> @@\\n`, body, and if `cut`\n  `SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(maxSection)`.\n- No binary detection: content is treated as text bytes. An empty file\n  yields `@@ -0,0 +1,0 @@` and no body.\n\n### 2.11 Branch survey scope (not ported)\n\nThe Go `branch` scope was unreachable from the CLI and is intentionally\nabsent from the TypeScript port (deliberate change 23, spec.md); this\nnumber is kept so later references stay stable.\n\n### 2.12 Repo survey scope (`scope: \"repo\"`)\n\nReviews the committed tree at HEAD as one snapshot.\n\n1. `status --porcelain` (failure -> `git status --porcelain: <cause>`).\n   If non-blank: if `linguistExcludes` non-empty, note `working tree is\n   dirty: skipping .gitattributes-based excludes for repo scope (they were\n   resolved from the mutable index/worktree, not HeadOID)`; set\n   `linguistExcludes = []`; then note `working tree has uncommitted\n   changes not reflected in this repo-scope snapshot (pinned to\n   HeadOID)`.\n2. If `rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD` succeeds: `headOID = rev-parse\n   HEAD` trimmed (failure -> `git rev-parse HEAD: <cause>`). Else note\n   `repository has no commits; manifest is empty`.\n3. With a `headOID`: `ls-tree -r --name-only -z <headOID>` (failure ->\n   `git ls-tree: <cause>`). Keep each decoded path (2.15; non-UTF-8 names\n   are skipped and counted) that is not in the\n   `linguistExcludes` set (exact membership, not glob) and that passes\n   `untrackedPasses(path, paths, allExcludes)`. Sort.\n   (Read from the HEAD tree, not the index, so staged changes do not leak\n   into or out of the listing.)\n4. `headLine`: with a `headOID`, `log --oneline --no-color\n   --no-show-signature -1` trimmed; errors ignored (empty).\n5. Top-level directory of each file = text before the first `/`, or the\n   whole name for a root file. Count per directory; sort directory names.\n6. Manifest, with `q(s)` = `manifestQuote(s)` (2.12.1) applied to the\n   head line, directory names, and file names:\n   ```\n   ### Repository snapshot ###\n   HEAD: <q(headLine)>            (or \"HEAD: (no commits)\" when empty)\n   <F> tracked file(s) across <D> top-level director(ies)\n\n   ### Files by top-level directory ###\n   <q(dir)> (<count>)             (one per directory)\n\n   ### Tracked files ###\n   <q(file)>                      (one per file)\n   ```\n   Every line ends in `\\n`. If any `q` call quoted, append\n   `\\n(Names in double quotes contain control or markup characters and\n   are Go-escaped; unescape one before passing it to a tool.)\\n`.\n7. Return `{manifest, survey:true, files, headOID, notes}`; `base`,\n   `baseOID`, `mergeBaseOID` empty.\n\n#### 2.12.1 manifestQuote\n\nA name is safe (returned unchanged) when it contains none\nof `\"`, `\\`, `<`, `>`, and every code point is printable per Go's\n`unicode.IsPrint`: categories L, M, N, P, S, plus U+0020 only (other\nspaces, controls, format characters, line/paragraph separators are not).\nOtherwise return Go `strconv.Quote(s)` with every `<` replaced by `\\x3c`\nand every `>` by `\\x3e`. `strconv.Quote` rules: wrap in `\"`; `\"` and `\\`\nescaped with `\\`; printable code points literal; `\\a \\b \\f \\n \\r \\t \\v`\nfor those controls; other code points below U+0020 and U+007F as `\\xNN`;\nother non-printable code points as\n`\\uXXXX` (< U+10000) or `\\UXXXXXXXX`. Hex digits are lowercase. Names\nreaching it are always valid UTF-8 (2.15), so Go's invalid-byte `\\xNN`\ncase does not arise. Unicode printable differences between Go's\n`unicode.IsPrint` and JS `\\p{...}` classes for newly assigned code points\nare accepted (deliberate change, section 4).\n\n### 2.13 gitRoot\n\n`gitRoot(start)`: `git -C <start> rev-parse --show-toplevel`, trimmed.\nAny failure -> error `not inside a git repository`. Used by every command\nthat needs the repo (review, init, trust, doctor, gate, skill, ignore,\nruns, config discovery).\n\n### 2.14 In-process glob matching (`untrackedPasses`)\n\nUsed for untracked files and the repo-scope listing (git's pathspec\nengine handles tracked diffs).\n\n`globToRegExp(pattern)` builds an anchored full-match regex scanning the\npattern byte by byte:\n\n- `**` -> `.*`, and if immediately followed by `/`, that `/` becomes `/?`;\n- `*` -> `.*` (crosses `/`, Python `fnmatch` semantics);\n- `?` -> `.` (one character);\n- each of `. + ^ $ { } ( ) [ ] |` and `\\` -> escaped literally (so `[...]`\n  classes are NOT supported);\n- anything else literal.\n\n`.` must not match `\\n` and must match every other character (Go RE2\nsemantics; in JS use `[^\\n]` rather than `.`).\n\n`globMatch(path, pattern)`: match `pattern`; if not, and replacing every\n`**` with `*` changes it, match that instead.\n\n`untrackedPasses(path, paths, excludes)`:\n\n1. Any exclude glob matches -> false.\n2. `paths` empty -> true.\n3. For each path pattern: glob match -> true; or, when the pattern\n   contains none of `*`, `?`, `[`, let `dir` = pattern without one\n   trailing `/`; `dir` non-empty and `path` starts with `dir + \"/\"` ->\n   true (a bare directory selects its subtree, as git pathspecs do).\n4. Otherwise false.\n\nExamples: `vendor/foo/bar.go` matches `vendor/**`; `a/node_modules/d.js`\nmatches `**/node_modules/**`; `foo.pb.go` matches `*.pb.go`; `keep.go`\nmatches neither `*.pb.go` nor `vendor/**`.\n\n### 2.15 Ordering and text rules\n\n- All sorts are bytewise over UTF-8 (equivalently by Unicode code point),\n  not JS's default UTF-16 order.\n- \"Non-empty lines\" = split on `\\n`, drop empty strings (no trimming).\n- NUL splitting: split on `\\0`, drop exactly one trailing empty element;\n  empty input -> no elements. It is done on the raw `Buffer`.\n- Path decoding (deliberate change 24): each NUL-split path from\n  `diff --name-only -z`, `ls-files --others -z`, `ls-files -z`, and\n  `ls-tree -z` is decoded with `new TextDecoder('utf-8', { fatal: true })`.\n  A path that is not valid UTF-8 is skipped and counted (it cannot\n  round-trip through a JS string). If the total count for the build is\n  non-zero, the build adds the note `skipped <N> path(s) whose names are\n  not valid UTF-8`, once, as its last note. Git's pathspec filtering has\n  already happened, so such a file's section may still appear in the diff\n  text; only the path is absent from `files`. Records from the rename\n  `--name-status -z` walk (2.8) skip invalid names silently.\n- \"Trim\" = remove leading/trailing whitespace (Go `strings.TrimSpace`).\n\n### 2.16 Fit (whole-diff budget)\n\n`fit(scope, maxBytes)` (called by the CLI after grounding has captured\nthe pre-fit diff):\n\n- `byteLength(diff) <= maxBytes` -> unchanged, no breakdown.\n- Else breakdown: for each file in `files`, `{path, bytes:\n  fileDiffBytes(file)}`, sorted by bytes descending with a stable sort:\n  ties keep `files` order (deliberate change 18; JS `Array.prototype.sort`\n  is stable). New diff = first `maxBytes` bytes + `\"\\n... (diff\n  truncated)\\n\"`; note `diff truncated to <maxBytes> bytes`.\n\n`fileDiffBytes(diff, P)`: starting at `pos = 0`, repeatedly find\n`diff --git a/P b/P` at or after `pos` (index `start`); the block ends at\n`end` = the index of the `\\n` that precedes the next `\"\\ndiff --git \"`\nfound searching from `start + 1`, or the end of the diff; add\n`end - start`; set `pos = end`. Then add the full length of every section\n(2.8 `splitSections`) whose `renameOf` yields `from === P` or `to === P`\n(a collapsed move's header names two paths, so it counts for both).\n\nA path that git C-quotes in `diff --git` headers (non-ASCII, tab, quote)\nnever matches the literal `a/P b/P` search, so its breakdown entry is 0\nbytes; this is accepted.\n\nThe CLI renders the breakdown in the report (render area).\n\n### 2.17 Error surface\n\n`build` errors are plain errors (never usage errors). The CLI prints them\nand maps uncategorized errors to its default operational exit code (CLI\narea). Best-effort steps (linguist, rename compaction, untracked\nsynthesis of one file, repo-scope head line) never fail the build, with\nthe exception of cancellation (3.3).\n\n## 3. TypeScript module design\n\n### 3.1 Files under `src/scope/`\n\n```\nsrc/scope/constants.ts  DEFAULT_IGNORES, PATCH_FLAGS, DIFF_FLAGS,\n                        DIFF_CONFIG, BASE_CANDIDATES, EMPTY_TREE,\n                        DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES, sectionOmittedMarker\nsrc/scope/git.ts        git subprocess helpers (execFile + spawn)\nsrc/scope/patch.ts      SectionCapper, sectionName, truncationNote,\n                        listSome\nsrc/scope/renames.ts    splitSections, classify, renameOf, compactRenames,\n                        renameNote\nsrc/scope/glob.ts       globToRegExp, globMatch, untrackedPasses\nsrc/scope/linguist.ts   linguistIgnores, linguistMarked\nsrc/scope/untracked.ts  synthesizeNewFileDiff\nsrc/scope/survey.ts     buildSurveyRepo, manifestQuote, goQuote\nsrc/scope/build.ts      build, isEmpty, fit, fileDiffBytes, gitRoot,\n                        resolveBase, buildRangeArgs, pathspecArgs\nsrc/scope/text.ts       compareBytes, nonEmptyLines, splitNul,\n                        decodePaths\n```\n\nDepends on `src/snapshot/` (`denylisted(path)`) only. Used by `src/cli/`\n(review, and `gitRoot` everywhere), `src/context/` (imports `PATCH_FLAGS` for\nits own `git diff --no-renames ... <range> -- <file>` calls, prefixed with\n`DIFF_CONFIG`), and indirectly by `src/record/` (the CLI copies scope fields\ninto `meta.json`'s `scope` object).\n\n### 3.2 Types and signatures\n\n```ts\nexport type ScopeKind = ''|'diff'|'repo'\n\nexport interface BuildOpts {\n    base?:string\n    uncommittedOnly?:boolean\n    stagedOnly?:boolean\n    paths?:readonly string[]\n    excludes?:readonly string[]\n    includeVendored?:boolean\n    scope?:string\n    stagedTreeOID?:string\n    maxFileDiffBytes?:number\n}\n\nexport interface Scope {\n    readonly diff:string\n    readonly diffBytes:Buffer\n    readonly files:readonly string[]\n    readonly base:string\n    readonly notes:readonly string[]\n    readonly staged:boolean","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-10-review-scope/4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4/tool-results/bph59j63h.txt","persistedOutputSize":44617},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Nr1CU1JsDKjfxrF1PnPBUY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChAwU4krYvEbhoRSwzyL8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GYYSDjzVRHZzsux3R9LGaS","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-10-review-scope/4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4/tool-results/bph59j63h.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1010,"cache_read_input_tokens":61082,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1010},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4","uuid":"8ddd184c-e8e6-45ef-83cb-34a10057d70d","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:23:58.265Z","request_id":"req_011ChAwU4Xxr9YLxKJAxfQMP","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GYYSDjzVRHZzsux3R9LGaS":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-10-review-scope/4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4/tool-results/bph59j63h.txt"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GYYSDjzVRHZzsux3R9LGaS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Area 04: Review scope\n2\t\n3\t## 1. Purpose\n4\t\n5\tThe scope area decides exactly what a review looks at. Given the Git\n6\ttoplevel and the user's scoping choices (`--staged`, `--base`,\n7\t`--uncommitted-only`, `--paths`, `--exclude`, `--include-vendored`,\n8\t`--scope`), it runs `git` and produces a **Scope**: the diff text the\n9\t**finder** reads (or, for repo scope, a small manifest), the sorted list of\n10\tfiles in scope, the resolved base, the commit/tree identities the rest of\n11\tthe review pins its reads to, and human-facing notes that explain every\n12\tscoping decision.\n13\t\n14\tTwo invariants drive the design:\n15\t\n16\t1. The file list is derived from the same pathspec-filtered git queries\n17\t   that produced the diff text. A file outside the post-exclude diff can\n18\t   never reach the finder, the **verifier**, or the report.\n19\t2. Nothing a repository or the user's Git config can do may run a program\n20\t   on, rewrite, or widen the reviewed content: no external diff drivers,\n21\t   no textconv, no global/system attribute files narrowing scope, no\n22\t   following symlinks out of the tree, and secret-looking untracked files\n23\t   are never sent.\n24\t\n25\tDownstream consumers: the CLI `review` command (prints notes, decides\n26\t\"no diff\", records scope into the **run record**), the context area\n27\t(grounding and file readers pin to `staged` / `stagedTreeOID` /\n28\t`headOID`), and the finder prompt (diff or manifest). The pre-commit\n29\t**gate** reaches this area through `review --staged`.\n30\t\n31\t## 2. Behavior contract\n32\t\n33\tAll byte counts are UTF-8 byte counts. All git commands below are run as `git\n34\t-C <repo> <args...>` with the parent environment inherited (so an ambient\n35\t`GIT_INDEX_FILE`, `GIT_DIR`, etc. is honored, see 2.6), stdout captured as a\n36\t`Buffer`, and stderr captured for error messages. `<repo>` is always the Git\n37\ttoplevel (from `gitRoot`, 2.13).\n38\t\n39\t### 2.1 Constants\n40\t\n41\t`DEFAULT_IGNORES` (order matters for argument order):\n42\t\n43\t```\n44\tvendor/**\n45\tnode_modules/**\n46\t**/node_modules/**\n47\t*.lock\n48\tpackage-lock.json\n49\tyarn.lock\n50\tpnpm-lock.yaml\n51\tgo.sum\n52\t*.pb.go\n53\t*_pb2.py\n54\t*_pb2_grpc.py\n55\t*.min.js\n56\t*.min.css\n57\t```\n58\t\n59\t`PATCH_FLAGS` go on every git invocation that produces a patch or a name\n60\tlist from `diff` (so a repo's `.gitattributes` diff driver, the user's\n61\t`diff.external`, color, and prefix settings cannot change the text):\n62\t\n63\t```\n64\t--no-ext-diff --no-textconv --no-color --src-prefix=a/ --dst-prefix=b/\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t`DIFF_FLAGS` = `--no-renames` followed by `PATCH_FLAGS`. Every diff-mode\n68\t`git diff` uses `DIFF_FLAGS`. Renames are recovered later (2.8) only for\n69\tpairs whose both sides survived the excludes.\n70\t\n71\t`DIFF_CONFIG` = `-c diff.orderFile= -c core.quotePath=false`, placed\n72\tbefore the subcommand. Every\n73\tgit invocation whose subcommand is `diff` in this file, and in the context\n74\tarea's own `git diff` calls, runs as `git -C <repo> DIFF_CONFIG diff ...`\n75\t(the argument lists below omit it for brevity). It overrides the user's\n76\t`diff.orderFile` so section order is always git's default path order\n77\t(deliberate change 17, spec.md). The empty value must be verified against\n78\tthe installed git during implementation (a test with a global\n79\t`diff.orderFile` that reverses the order, 5). If git rejects an empty\n80\tvalue or still applies the file, use `-c diff.orderFile=/dev/null`\n81\tinstead; the contract is only that the user's order file has no effect.\n82\t`core.quotePath=false` keeps non-ASCII paths unquoted in patch headers\n83\t(`diff --git a/... b/...`), so the fit breakdown attributes their bytes\n84\tto the right file (deliberate change 15); git still quotes paths with\n85\ttabs, newlines, or double quotes, and those report 0 bytes in the\n86\tbreakdown as in Go.\n87\t\n88\t`BASE_CANDIDATES`, tried in order when no `--base` is given (diff scope\n89\tonly): `origin/main`, `origin/master`, `main`, `master`, `HEAD~1`.\n90\t\n91\t`EMPTY_TREE` = `4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904` (git's empty\n92\ttree; lets a repo with no commits be diffed).\n93\t\n94\t`DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES` = `1_000_000`.\n95\t\n96\t`SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(n)` is exactly this single line (with `n` in\n97\tdecimal, ending in a newline; it exceeds 80 columns by necessity):\n98\t\n99\t```\n100\t\\ roast: the rest of this file's diff is omitted (over <n> bytes); use read_file to inspect the file\n101\t```\n102\t\n103\tThe leading `\\` makes it diff metadata (like git's `\\ No newline at end\n104\tof file`), so the grounder and rename parser skip it as content.\n105\t\n106\t`LINGUIST_ATTRS` = `linguist-generated`, `linguist-vendored`.\n107\t\n108\t### 2.2 Output: the Scope value\n109\t\n110\t- `diff` -- combined diff text: `diffBytes` decoded once as UTF-8 with\n111\t  U+FFFD replacement, for models and reports. Empty in repo scope.\n112\t- `files` -- sorted (bytewise, 2.15) unique repo-relative paths in scope.\n113\t- `base` -- resolved base ref *name* (e.g. `origin/main`), not an OID.\n114\t  Empty for uncommitted-only, staged, no-usable-base, and repo scope.\n115\t- `diffBytes` -- the same combined diff as a `Buffer`: the exact bytes git\n116\t  and the untracked reader produced (after capping), so anything hashed or\n117\t  recorded is byte-exact (cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\", spec.md).\n118\t- `notes` -- human-facing scoping notes, in the order generated. The CLI\n119\t  prints each to stderr.\n120\t- `staged` -- true only for `stagedOnly`; file context must come from the\n121\t  index (or the pinned tree), not the worktree.\n122\t- `stagedTreeOID` -- echo of `BuildOpts.stagedTreeOID` in staged mode;\n123\t  else empty.\n124\t- `manifest` -- survey map (repo scope); empty in diff scope.\n125\t- `survey` -- true for `repo` scope.\n126\t- `headOID` -- repo scope: `rev-parse HEAD`; empty if no commits.\n127\t  Empty in diff scope.\n128\t\n129\t`isEmpty(scope)`: in repo scope, true when `files` is empty; otherwise\n130\ttrue when `diff` is empty after trimming whitespace. The CLI then writes\n131\ta report with assessment `NO_DIFF` and exits 0 (CLI area).\n132\t\n133\t### 2.3 Inputs: BuildOpts and how the CLI fills them\n134\t\n135\t- `base` -- `--base` (string, default `\"\"`).\n136\t- `uncommittedOnly` -- `--uncommitted-only`.\n137\t- `stagedOnly` -- `--staged`.\n138\t- `paths` -- `--paths` (string slice: comma-separated and repeatable).\n139\t- `excludes` -- `.roast.json` `review.excludes`, followed by `--exclude`\n140\t  values.\n141\t- `includeVendored` -- `--include-vendored`.\n142\t- `scope` -- `--scope` (`diff` default; the CLI accepts only `diff` or\n143\t  `repo`).\n144\t- `stagedTreeOID` -- with `--staged`, the OID from the context area's\n145\t  `pinIndexTree(repo)`, computed before `build`.\n146\t- `maxFileDiffBytes` -- the whole-diff budget: `limits.max_diff_bytes`,\n147\t  else 1_000_000, or 200_000 with `--quick`.\n148\t\n149\t`maxFileDiffBytes <= 0` (or absent) means `DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES`.\n150\tBelow this, `maxSection` names the effective value.\n151\t\n152\tCLI-level validation (owned by the CLI area, restated for context):\n153\t`--scope` other than `diff`/`repo` is a usage error; `--scope repo` with\n154\t`--quick` is a usage error; `--scope repo` with any of `--staged`,\n155\t`--uncommitted-only`, `--base` is a usage error. `build` itself accepts\n156\t`\"\"`, `diff`, `repo` and rejects anything else with\n157\t`unsupported scope \"<x>\": expected \"\", \"diff\", or \"repo\"`.\n158\t`--staged` together with `--uncommitted-only` or `--base` is not rejected;\n159\tstaged mode simply ignores both.\n160\t\n161\t### 2.4 Common prelude (all scopes)\n162\t\n163\t1. `allExcludes` = `opts.excludes`, then (unless `includeVendored`)\n164\t   `DEFAULT_IGNORES` appended.\n165\t2. Unless `includeVendored`: `linguistExcludes = linguistIgnores(repo,\n166\t   cached = stagedOnly, treeOID = stagedTreeOID)` (2.7). If non-empty,\n167\t   note `excluding <N> path(s) marked linguist-generated/-vendored in\n168\t   .gitattributes (--include-vendored to review)`.\n169\t3. `spec = pathspecArgs(paths, allExcludes, linguistExcludes)`:\n170\t   - if all three lists are empty: no arguments at all;\n171\t   - else `--`, then each path verbatim (git pathspec, user magic allowed),\n172\t     then `:(exclude)<pattern>` per `allExcludes` entry, then\n173\t     `:(exclude,literal)<path>` per `linguistExcludes` entry.\n174\t4. Dispatch on `scope`: `repo` -> 2.12, `\"\"`/`diff` -> 2.5, anything else\n175\t   -> the `unsupported scope` error.\n176\t\n177\tThe linguist step runs before dispatch, so it also runs (uncached) for\n178\trepo scope.\n179\t\n180\t### 2.5 Diff scope\n181\t\n182\t**No-commit detection.** `head = \"HEAD\"`. Run `rev-parse --verify --quiet\n183\tHEAD` (stdout/stderr discarded). On non-zero exit: `head = EMPTY_TREE`,\n184\tforce `uncommittedOnly = true`, note `repository has no commits;\n185\treviewing staged + untracked files`.\n186\t\n187\t**Staged mode** (`stagedOnly`), what a commit would contain:\n188\t\n189\t1. `stagedRef` = `[\"--cached\", head]`, or `[head, stagedTreeOID]` when\n190\t   `stagedTreeOID` is set (the pinned tree replaces the live index; same\n191\t   comparison, immutable OID).\n192\t2. Patch: `diff DIFF_FLAGS stagedRef spec` through the section capper\n193\t   (2.9). Failure -> error `git diff (staged): <cause>`.\n194\t3. `notes = truncationNote(notes, cut, maxSection)`.\n195\t4. `diff, n = compactRenames(stagedRef, spec, diff)`; `renameNote(n)`.\n196\t5. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z stagedRef spec`. Failure ->\n197\t   error `git diff --name-only (staged): <cause>`. `files` = the decoded\n198\t   paths (NUL-split, 2.15), sorted.\n199\t6. Return `{diff, files, notes, staged:true, stagedTreeOID}`; `base`\n200\t   empty. No untracked files, no worktree content.\n201\t\n202\t**Default mode** (committed-since-base + uncommitted + untracked):\n203\t\n204\t1. Unless `uncommittedOnly`:\n205\t   1. `resolvedBase = resolveBase(base)`: candidates are `[base]` if given,\n206\t      else `BASE_CANDIDATES`; the first for which `rev-parse --verify\n207\t      --quiet <ref>` exits 0 wins; none -> `\"\"`.\n208\t   2. If `\"\"`: note `no usable base ref (tried <T>); reviewing uncommitted\n209\t      changes only` where `<T>` is the requested base, or\n210\t      `BASE_CANDIDATES.join(\", \")`. Skip to step 2.\n211\t   3. If no base was requested and `resolvedBase !== \"origin/main\"`: note\n212\t      `using base '<resolvedBase>'`.\n213\t   4. `rangeArgs = buildRangeArgs(resolvedBase)`: run `merge-base <base>\n214\t      HEAD` (output discarded); exit 0 -> `[\"<base>...HEAD\"]`, else\n215\t      `[\"<base>\", \"HEAD\"]`.\n216\t   5. Patch: `diff DIFF_FLAGS rangeArgs spec` through the capper. Failure ->\n217\t      `git diff (committed): <cause>`. Cut names go to `cutSections`.\n218\t   6. `committed, n = compactRenames(rangeArgs, spec, committed)`;\n219\t      `renameNote(n)`.\n220\t   7. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z rangeArgs spec`. Failure ->\n221\t      `git diff --name-only (committed): <cause>`.\n222\t2. Uncommitted (worktree and index vs `head`): patch `diff DIFF_FLAGS\n223\t   <head> spec` through the capper; failure -> `git diff (uncommitted):\n224\t   <cause>`; cut names appended to `cutSections`; `compactRenames([head],\n225\t   spec, ...)`; `renameNote`. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z\n226\t   <head> spec`; failure -> `git diff --name-only (uncommitted): <cause>`.\n227\t3. `diff = committed`; if `uncommitted.trim() !== \"\"`, append\n228\t   `\"\\n\\n### Uncommitted changes ###\\n\" + uncommitted` (the separator is\n229\t   added even when `committed` is empty).\n230\t4. `fileSet` = the decoded paths of both name lists (2.15).\n231\t5. Untracked files (2.10) are appended to `diff` and `fileSet`.\n232\t6. `notes = truncationNote(notes, cutSections, maxSection)` -- one note for\n233\t   committed, uncommitted, and untracked cuts together, emitted after the\n234\t   untracked denylist note.\n235\t7. Return `{diff, files: sorted fileSet, base: resolvedBase, notes}`.\n236\t\n237\tName lists use `-z` (deliberate change 15): paths arrive NUL-separated and\n238\tverbatim, never C-quoted, so a path with non-ASCII, tab, or quote characters\n239\tis a normal entry in `files`. Patch text still C-quotes such paths in its\n240\t`diff --git` headers (see 2.8, 2.16). Binary files appear as git prints them\n241\twithout `--binary` or `--text` (`Binary files a/x and b/x differ`); there is\n242\tno other binary handling for tracked files.\n243\t\n244\t### 2.6 Ambient Git environment\n245\t\n246\tGit subprocesses inherit the full parent environment. In particular a hook\n247\tinvoked by `git commit <paths>` sets `GIT_INDEX_FILE` to an alternate\n248\tindex; staged mode must review that index, not `.git/index`. (The pinned\n249\ttree from `pinIndexTree` already honors it; the `--cached` fallback does\n250\ttoo by inheritance.)\n251\t\n252\t### 2.7 Linguist generated/vendored exclusion\n253\t\n254\tA repo's in-tree `.gitattributes` may mark paths `linguist-generated` or\n255\t`linguist-vendored`; those are treated like `DEFAULT_IGNORES` and are\n256\treviewed only with `--include-vendored`.\n257\t\n258\t**`linguistIgnores(repo, cached, treeOID)`**, best-effort (every failure\n259\treturns no excludes, so the review proceeds unfiltered -- never narrower):\n260\t\n261\t1. Pinned index: only when `cached && treeOID`: create a temp dir\n262\t   (`<tmpdir>/roast-scope-idx-*`), `idx = <dir>/index`, extra env\n263\t   `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<idx>`, run `read-tree <treeOID>` with that env.\n264\t   Failure -> remove the dir, return no excludes (do NOT fall back to the\n265\t   live index). The temp dir is removed when the function returns.\n266\t2. `ls-files -z` (with the extra env) -> NUL-split paths, decoded per 2.15\n267\t   (invalid names skipped, counted). Failure -> none.\n268\t3. Only when `cached`: also `diff DIFF_FLAGS --cached --name-only -z`\n269\t   (with the extra env; `DIFF_FLAGS` includes `--no-renames`, deliberate\n270\t   change 16); on success append its paths. With renames off, a staged\n271\t   rename lists both its old and new path, so a staged deletion or rename\n272\t   source of a marked file, absent from the index, is still excluded.\n273\t   Failure here is ignored.\n274\t4. No paths -> none. Else `linguistMarked(paths, cached, env)`, sorted.\n275\t\n276\t**`linguistMarked(repo, paths, cached, env)`**:\n277\t\n278\t- Empty `paths` -> none.\n279\t- Command: `git -C <repo> -c core.attributesFile=/dev/null check-attr\n280\t  --stdin -z [--cached] linguist-generated linguist-vendored`.\n281\t- Environment: parent env, plus `GIT_ATTR_NOSYSTEM=1`, plus `env` last\n282\t  (so a pinned `GIT_INDEX_FILE` wins). These two drop the user-global and\n283\t  system attribute files; only in-tree/index attributes (and\n284\t  `$GIT_DIR/info/attributes`, which cannot be disabled) can narrow scope.\n285\t- Stdin: `paths.join(\"\\0\") + \"\\0\"`.\n286\t- Any failure -> none.\n287\t- Output is NUL-separated `(path, attr, value)` triples; a short trailing\n288\t  group is ignored. A path is marked when `value` is `set` or `true`\n289\t  (`unspecified`, `unset`, `false` do not count, so\n290\t  `dir/x linguist-generated=false` un-marks). Deduplicate, keeping first\n291\t  occurrence order.\n292\t\n293\t**Attribute source per mode**: staged -> index (`--cached`), pinned to\n294\t`stagedTreeOID` when set; every other mode -> worktree `.gitattributes`.\n295\tUntracked candidates always use the worktree (`cached = false`, no pinned\n296\tenv), regardless of mode.\n297\t\n298\tLinguist excludes are exact paths and go into pathspecs with\n299\t`:(exclude,literal)` magic, so a file literally named `*.go` excludes only\n300\titself.\n301\t\n302\t### 2.8 Rename compaction\n303\t\n304\tDiffs use `--no-renames` so excludes apply to both sides of a move.\n305\t`compactRenames(repo, ref, spec, diffText, maxSection)` then collapses\n306\tmoves whose two sides both survived the excludes. It returns\n307\t`(diffText', pairsCollapsed)` and on any git error returns the input\n308\tunchanged with 0.\n309\t\n310\t**Sections.** `splitSections(text)`: starting at offset 0, each section\n311\truns up to and including the `\\n` before the next occurrence of\n312\t`\"\\ndiff --git \"` searched from `start + 1`; the last runs to the end.\n313\tLeading text before the first header is its own section.\n314\t`classify(section)`:\n315\t\n316\t- Header = first line. It must start with `diff --git `; the remainder\n317\t  `pair` must not start with `\"`, must have `len >= 7` and\n318\t  `(len - 5)` even; with `n = (len - 5) / 2`, `path = pair[2 .. 2+n)`,\n319\t  require `pair[0..2) == \"a/\"` and `pair[2+n..) == \" b/\" + path`.\n320\t  Otherwise kind `other`.\n321\t- Scan the following lines: the first of `@@...` or `--- ...` -> `other`;\n322\t  `deleted file mode ...` -> `deleted`; `new file mode ...` -> `added`.\n323\t\n324\t**Algorithm.**\n325\t\n326\t1. Collect `deleted` and `added` paths. Either empty -> unchanged, 0.\n327\t2. `diff -M PATCH_FLAGS --name-status -z <ref...> <spec...>` (failure ->\n328\t   unchanged). Walk NUL fields as records: a status field starting with\n329\t   `R` or `C` takes two paths (`from`, `to`), any other status one path\n330\t   (advance 2). A truncated R/C record stops the walk. Accept `(from, to)`\n331\t   only when status starts with `R`, `from` is a deleted path and `to` is\n332\t   an added path of this diff. (Walking whole records is required: a path\n333\t   like `README.md` must never be read as a status.)\n334\t3. No accepted pairs -> unchanged.\n335\t4. `diff -M PATCH_FLAGS <ref...> <spec...>` through the section capper with\n336\t   the same `maxSection` (its cut list is discarded; failure ->\n337\t   unchanged).\n338\t5. For each section of that output, `renameOf(section)`: scan lines until\n339\t   one starts with `@@`; take values of `rename from ` and `rename to `\n340\t   lines; reject when either is empty or starts with `\"` (quoted paths are\n341\t   left uncollapsed). Keep the section when `(from, to)` is accepted; mark\n342\t   both paths `paired`; append the section text, plus `\\n` if it does not\n343\t   end in one.\n344\t6. No paired paths -> unchanged.\n345\t7. Output: every original section in order except `deleted`/`added`\n346\t   sections whose path is paired; then a `\\n` if the output is non-empty\n347\t   and does not end in one; then all kept rename sections. Count =\n348\t   `paired.size / 2`.\n349\t\n350\t`renameNote(n)` for `n > 0`: `collapsed <n> moved file(s) to git's rename\n351\tform (both paths in scope)`. A pure move therefore has a rename header and\n352\tno hunks; a move with a small edit shows only the edited lines.\n353\t\n354\t### 2.9 Patch production and per-file cap\n355\t\n356\t`gitPatch(repo, args, maxSection)` runs git and streams stdout through a\n357\tsection capper, so memory per file is bounded by `maxSection` (a 24 MB\n358\tfile must not be buffered whole). Algorithm on the byte stream, processed\n359\tline by line (a line longer than the read buffer arrives in fragments;\n360\tonly a fragment at line start can be a header):\n361\t\n362\t- State: `header = \"\"`, `used = 0`, `dropping = false`.\n363\t- A fragment at line start beginning with `diff --git ` first finishes the\n364\t  previous section, then sets `header` to that line (without newline),\n365\t  `used = 0`, `dropping = false`.\n366\t- While not dropping, keep `take = min(len, maxSection - used)` bytes of\n367\t  the fragment; if `take < len`, start dropping.\n368\t- Finishing a section that was dropping: if the output does not end in\n369\t  `\\n`, add one; append `SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(maxSection)`; push\n370\t  `sectionName(header)` to `cut`.\n371\t- At EOF, finish the last section.\n372\t- Text before the first header counts toward a section named `\"\"`.\n373\t- Non-zero git exit -> error `exit status <code>: <trimmed stderr>`; a\n374\t  read error kills git and reports the read error.\n375\t\n376\t`sectionName(header)`: strip `diff --git `; if it is `a/P b/P` (same\n377\tlength rule as `classify`), return `P`; else the raw remainder.\n378\t\n379\t`truncationNote(cut, maxSection)` for non-empty `cut`: `diff for <N>\n380\tfile(s) cut at <maxSection> bytes each: <listSome(cut, 10)>`.\n381\t`listSome(items, 10)`: up to 10 joined by `, `; beyond 10, the first 10\n382\tthen `, and <K> more`.\n383\t\n384\tThe cut is at a byte offset and may split a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence;\n385\tsee 3.4 for decoding.\n386\t\n387\t### 2.10 Untracked files (default diff mode only)\n388\t\n389\t1. `ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z` (deliberate change 15);\n390\t   failure -> error `git ls-files --others: <cause>`.\n391\t2. For each decoded path `u` (2.15; names that are not valid UTF-8 are\n392\t   skipped and counted), in git's order:\n393\t   - skip unless `untrackedPasses(u, paths, allExcludes)` (2.14);\n394\t   - if `snapshot.denylisted(u)` -> add to `denied` (the snapshot area's\n395\t     secret denylist; applies even with `--include-vendored`);\n396\t   - else candidate.\n397\t3. `denied` non-empty -> note `skipped <N> untracked file(s) matching the\n398\t   secret denylist: <listSome(denied, 10)>`.\n399\t4. Unless `includeVendored`, drop candidates returned by\n400\t   `linguistMarked(repo, candidates, cached=false, env=none)`.\n401\t5. For each remaining candidate, `synthesizeNewFileDiff(u, maxSection)`;\n402\t   empty result -> skipped silently (not added to `files`); cut -> add `u`\n403\t   to `cutSections`; else add the block and add `u` to `fileSet`.\n404\t6. If any blocks: if `diff.trim() !== \"\"` append `\"\\n\\n### Untracked files\n405\t   (treated as new-file additions) ###\\n\"`; then append\n406\t   `blocks.join(\"\\n\")`.\n407\t\n408\t**`synthesizeNewFileDiff(relpath, maxSection)`**:\n409\t\n410\t- `lstat(<repo>/<relpath>)`; not a regular file (symlink, dir, missing,\n411\t  error) -> `\"\"`. Symlinks are never followed.\n412\t- Read at most `maxSection + 1` bytes; any error -> `\"\"`. `cut =\n413\t  bytesRead > maxSection`.\n414\t- Split on `\\n`; drop one trailing empty element.\n415\t- `header = \"diff --git a/P b/P\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++\n416\t  b/P\\n\"` with `P = relpath` verbatim (no quoting).\n417\t- For each line: if `len(header) + len(body) + len(line) + 2 >\n418\t  maxSection`, set `cut` and stop; else append `\"+\" + line + \"\\n\"` and\n419\t  count it.\n420\t- Result: header, `@@ -0,0 +1,<count> @@\\n`, body, and if `cut`\n421\t  `SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(maxSection)`.\n422\t- No binary detection: content is treated as text bytes. An empty file\n423\t  yields `@@ -0,0 +1,0 @@` and no body.\n424\t\n425\t### 2.11 Branch survey scope (not ported)\n426\t\n427\tThe Go `branch` scope was unreachable from the CLI and is intentionally\n428\tabsent from the TypeScript port (deliberate change 23, spec.md); this\n429\tnumber is kept so later references stay stable.\n430\t\n431\t### 2.12 Repo survey scope (`scope: \"repo\"`)\n432\t\n433\tReviews the committed tree at HEAD as one snapshot.\n434\t\n435\t1. `status --porcelain` (failure -> `git status --porcelain: <cause>`).\n436\t   If non-blank: if `linguistExcludes` non-empty, note `working tree is\n437\t   dirty: skipping .gitattributes-based excludes for repo scope (they were\n438\t   resolved from the mutable index/worktree, not HeadOID)`; set\n439\t   `linguistExcludes = []`; then note `working tree has uncommitted\n440\t   changes not reflected in this repo-scope snapshot (pinned to\n441\t   HeadOID)`.\n442\t2. If `rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD` succeeds: `headOID = rev-parse\n443\t   HEAD` trimmed (failure -> `git rev-parse HEAD: <cause>`). Else note\n444\t   `repository has no commits; manifest is empty`.\n445\t3. With a `headOID`: `ls-tree -r --name-only -z <headOID>` (failure ->\n446\t   `git ls-tree: <cause>`). Keep each decoded path (2.15; non-UTF-8 names\n447\t   are skipped and counted) that is not in the\n448\t   `linguistExcludes` set (exact membership, not glob) and that passes\n449\t   `untrackedPasses(path, paths, allExcludes)`. Sort.\n450\t   (Read from the HEAD tree, not the index, so staged changes do not leak\n451\t   into or out of the listing.)\n452\t4. `headLine`: with a `headOID`, `log --oneline --no-color\n453\t   --no-show-signature -1` trimmed; errors ignored (empty).\n454\t5. Top-level directory of each file = text before the first `/`, or the\n455\t   whole name for a root file. Count per directory; sort directory names.\n456\t6. Manifest, with `q(s)` = `manifestQuote(s)` (2.12.1) applied to the\n457\t   head line, directory names, and file names:\n458\t   ```\n459\t   ### Repository snapshot ###\n460\t   HEAD: <q(headLine)>            (or \"HEAD: (no commits)\" when empty)\n461\t   <F> tracked file(s) across <D> top-level director(ies)\n462\t\n463\t   ### Files by top-level directory ###\n464\t   <q(dir)> (<count>)             (one per directory)\n465\t\n466\t   ### Tracked files ###\n467\t   <q(file)>                      (one per file)\n468\t   ```\n469\t   Every line ends in `\\n`. If any `q` call quoted, append\n470\t   `\\n(Names in double quotes contain control or markup characters and\n471\t   are Go-escaped; unescape one before passing it to a tool.)\\n`.\n472\t7. Return `{manifest, survey:true, files, headOID, notes}`; `base`,\n473\t   `baseOID`, `mergeBaseOID` empty.\n474\t\n475\t#### 2.12.1 manifestQuote\n476\t\n477\tA name is safe (returned unchanged) when it contains none\n478\tof `\"`, `\\`, `<`, `>`, and every code point is printable per Go's\n479\t`unicode.IsPrint`: categories L, M, N, P, S, plus U+0020 only (other\n480\tspaces, controls, format characters, line/paragraph separators are not).\n481\tOtherwise return Go `strconv.Quote(s)` with every `<` replaced by `\\x3c`\n482\tand every `>` by `\\x3e`. `strconv.Quote` rules: wrap in `\"`; `\"` and `\\`\n483\tescaped with `\\`; printable code points literal; `\\a \\b \\f \\n \\r \\t \\v`\n484\tfor those controls; other code points below U+0020 and U+007F as `\\xNN`;\n485\tother non-printable code points as\n486\t`\\uXXXX` (< U+10000) or `\\UXXXXXXXX`. Hex digits are lowercase. Names\n487\treaching it are always valid UTF-8 (2.15), so Go's invalid-byte `\\xNN`\n488\tcase does not arise. Unicode printable differences between Go's\n489\t`unicode.IsPrint` and JS `\\p{...}` classes for newly assigned code points\n490\tare accepted (deliberate change, section 4).\n491\t\n492\t### 2.13 gitRoot\n493\t\n494\t`gitRoot(start)`: `git -C <start> rev-parse --show-toplevel`, trimmed.\n495\tAny failure -> error `not inside a git repository`. Used by every command\n496\tthat needs the repo (review, init, trust, doctor, gate, skill, ignore,\n497\truns, config discovery).\n498\t\n499\t### 2.14 In-process glob matching (`untrackedPasses`)\n500\t\n501\tUsed for untracked files and the repo-scope listing (git's pathspec\n502\tengine handles tracked diffs).\n503\t\n504\t`globToRegExp(pattern)` builds an anchored full-match regex scanning the\n505\tpattern byte by byte:\n506\t\n507\t- `**` -> `.*`, and if immediately followed by `/`, that `/` becomes `/?`;\n508\t- `*` -> `.*` (crosses `/`, Python `fnmatch` semantics);\n509\t- `?` -> `.` (one character);\n510\t- each of `. + ^ $ { } ( ) [ ] |` and `\\` -> escaped literally (so `[...]`\n511\t  classes are NOT supported);\n512\t- anything else literal.\n513\t\n514\t`.` must not match `\\n` and must match every other character (Go RE2\n515\tsemantics; in JS use `[^\\n]` rather than `.`).\n516\t\n517\t`globMatch(path, pattern)`: match `pattern`; if not, and replacing every\n518\t`**` with `*` changes it, match that instead.\n519\t\n520\t`untrackedPasses(path, paths, excludes)`:\n521\t\n522\t1. Any exclude glob matches -> false.\n523\t2. `paths` empty -> true.\n524\t3. For each path pattern: glob match -> true; or, when the pattern\n525\t   contains none of `*`, `?`, `[`, let `dir` = pattern without one\n526\t   trailing `/`; `dir` non-empty and `path` starts with `dir + \"/\"` ->\n527\t   true (a bare directory selects its subtree, as git pathspecs do).\n528\t4. Otherwise false.\n529\t\n530\tExamples: `vendor/foo/bar.go` matches `vendor/**`; `a/node_modules/d.js`\n531\tmatches `**/node_modules/**`; `foo.pb.go` matches `*.pb.go`; `keep.go`\n532\tmatches neither `*.pb.go` nor `vendor/**`.\n533\t\n534\t### 2.15 Ordering and text rules\n535\t\n536\t- All sorts are bytewise over UTF-8 (equivalently by Unicode code point),\n537\t  not JS's default UTF-16 order.\n538\t- \"Non-empty lines\" = split on `\\n`, drop empty strings (no trimming).\n539\t- NUL splitting: split on `\\0`, drop exactly one trailing empty element;\n540\t  empty input -> no elements. It is done on the raw `Buffer`.\n541\t- Path decoding (deliberate change 24): each NUL-split path from\n542\t  `diff --name-only -z`, `ls-files --others -z`, `ls-files -z`, and\n543\t  `ls-tree -z` is decoded with `new TextDecoder('utf-8', { fatal: true })`.\n544\t  A path that is not valid UTF-8 is skipped and counted (it cannot\n545\t  round-trip through a JS string). If the total count for the build is\n546\t  non-zero, the build adds the note `skipped <N> path(s) whose names are\n547\t  not valid UTF-8`, once, as its last note. Git's pathspec filtering has\n548\t  already happened, so such a file's section may still appear in the diff\n549\t  text; only the path is absent from `files`. Records from the rename\n550\t  `--name-status -z` walk (2.8) skip invalid names silently.\n551\t- \"Trim\" = remove leading/trailing whitespace (Go `strings.TrimSpace`).\n552\t\n553\t### 2.16 Fit (whole-diff budget)\n554\t\n555\t`fit(scope, maxBytes)` (called by the CLI after grounding has captured\n556\tthe pre-fit diff):\n557\t\n558\t- `byteLength(diff) <= maxBytes` -> unchanged, no breakdown.\n559\t- Else breakdown: for each file in `files`, `{path, bytes:\n560\t  fileDiffBytes(file)}`, sorted by bytes descending with a stable sort:\n561\t  ties keep `files` order (deliberate change 18; JS `Array.prototype.sort`\n562\t  is stable). New diff = first `maxBytes` bytes + `\"\\n... (diff\n563\t  truncated)\\n\"`; note `diff truncated to <maxBytes> bytes`.\n564\t\n565\t`fileDiffBytes(diff, P)`: starting at `pos = 0`, repeatedly find\n566\t`diff --git a/P b/P` at or after `pos` (index `start`); the block ends at\n567\t`end` = the index of the `\\n` that precedes the next `\"\\ndiff --git \"`\n568\tfound searching from `start + 1`, or the end of the diff; add\n569\t`end - start`; set `pos = end`. Then add the full length of every section\n570\t(2.8 `splitSections`) whose `renameOf` yields `from === P` or `to === P`\n571\t(a collapsed move's header names two paths, so it counts for both).\n572\t\n573\tA path that git C-quotes in `diff --git` headers (non-ASCII, tab, quote)\n574\tnever matches the literal `a/P b/P` search, so its breakdown entry is 0\n575\tbytes; this is accepted.\n576\t\n577\tThe CLI renders the breakdown in the report (render area).\n578\t\n579\t### 2.17 Error surface\n580\t\n581\t`build` errors are plain errors (never usage errors). The CLI prints them\n582\tand maps uncategorized errors to its default operational exit code (CLI\n583\tarea). Best-effort steps (linguist, rename compaction, untracked\n584\tsynthesis of one file, repo-scope head line) never fail the build, with\n585\tthe exception of cancellation (3.3).\n586\t\n587\t## 3. TypeScript module design\n588\t\n589\t### 3.1 Files under `src/scope/`\n590\t\n591\t```\n592\tsrc/scope/constants.ts  DEFAULT_IGNORES, PATCH_FLAGS, DIFF_FLAGS,\n593\t                        DIFF_CONFIG, BASE_CANDIDATES, EMPTY_TREE,\n594\t                        DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES, sectionOmittedMarker\n595\tsrc/scope/git.ts        git subprocess helpers (execFile + spawn)\n596\tsrc/scope/patch.ts      SectionCapper, sectionName, truncationNote,\n597\t                        listSome\n598\tsrc/scope/renames.ts    splitSections, classify, renameOf, compactRenames,\n599\t                        renameNote\n600\tsrc/scope/glob.ts       globToRegExp, globMatch, untrackedPasses\n601\tsrc/scope/linguist.ts   linguistIgnores, linguistMarked\n602\tsrc/scope/untracked.ts  synthesizeNewFileDiff\n603\tsrc/scope/survey.ts     buildSurveyRepo, manifestQuote, goQuote\n604\tsrc/scope/build.ts      build, isEmpty, fit, fileDiffBytes, gitRoot,\n605\t                        resolveBase, buildRangeArgs, pathspecArgs\n606\tsrc/scope/text.ts       compareBytes, nonEmptyLines, splitNul,\n607\t                        decodePaths\n608\t```\n609\t\n610\tDepends on `src/snapshot/` (`denylisted(path)`) only. Used by `src/cli/`\n611\t(review, and `gitRoot` everywhere), `src/context/` (imports `PATCH_FLAGS` for\n612\tits own `git diff --no-renames ... <range> -- <file>` calls, prefixed with\n613\t`DIFF_CONFIG`), and indirectly by `src/record/` (the CLI copies scope fields\n614\tinto `meta.json`'s `scope` object).\n615\t\n616\t### 3.2 Types and signatures\n617\t\n618\t```ts\n619\texport type ScopeKind = ''|'diff'|'repo'\n620\t\n621\texport interface BuildOpts {\n622\t    base?:string\n623\t    uncommittedOnly?:boolean\n624\t    stagedOnly?:boolean\n625\t    paths?:readonly string[]\n626\t    excludes?:readonly string[]\n627\t    includeVendored?:boolean\n628\t    scope?:string\n629\t    stagedTreeOID?:string\n630\t    maxFileDiffBytes?:number\n631\t}\n632\t\n633\texport interface Scope {\n634\t    readonly diff:string\n635\t    readonly diffBytes:Buffer\n636\t    readonly files:readonly string[]\n637\t    readonly base:string\n638\t    readonly notes:readonly string[]\n639\t    readonly staged:boolean\n640\t    readonly stagedTreeOID:string\n641\t    readonly manifest:string\n642\t    readonly survey:boolean\n643\t    readonly headOID:string\n644\t}\n645\t\n646\texport interface SizeEntry {\n647\t    path:string\n648\t    bytes:number\n649\t}\n650\t\n651\texport function build (\n652\t    repo:string,\n653\t    opts:BuildOpts,\n654\t    signal?:AbortSignal\n655\t):Promise<Scope>\n656\t\n657\texport function isEmpty (scope:Scope):boolean\n658\t\n659\t// Pure; returns a new Scope rather than mutating (the CLI keeps the\n660\t// pre-fit diff for grounding).\n661\texport function fit (\n662\t    scope:Scope,\n663\t    maxBytes:number\n664\t):{ scope:Scope, breakdown:SizeEntry[]|null }\n665\t\n666\texport function gitRoot (\n667\t    start:string,\n668\t    signal?:AbortSignal\n669\t):Promise<string>\n670\t```\n671\t\n672\t### 3.3 git.ts\n673\t\n674\t```ts\n675\texport class GitError extends Error {\n676\t    // message: `exit status <code>: <trimmed stderr>`\n677\t    exitCode:number|null\n678\t    stderr:string\n679\t}\n680\t\n681\texport interface GitRunOpts {\n682\t    signal?:AbortSignal\n683\t    env?:Record<string, string>   // merged over process.env\n684\t    input?:Buffer|string          // written to stdin, then closed\n685\t}\n686\t\n687\t// Buffered: execFile('git', ['-C', repo, ...args], {\n688\t//   encoding: 'buffer', maxBuffer: Infinity, signal,\n689\t//   env: { ...process.env, ...opts.env } })\n690\texport function gitOut (\n691\t    repo:string, args:readonly string[], opts?:GitRunOpts\n692\t):Promise<Buffer>\n693\t\n694\t// Exit status only; stdout/stderr ignored. Resolves false on non-zero\n695\t// exit, rejects only on abort or spawn failure (ENOENT).\n696\texport function gitOk (\n697\t    repo:string, args:readonly string[], opts?:GitRunOpts\n698\t):Promise<boolean>\n699\t\n700\t// Streaming: spawn('git', ...) with { signal }, stdout piped through a\n701\t// SectionCapper chunk by chunk; stderr buffered.\n702\texport function gitPatch (\n703\t    repo:string, args:readonly string[], maxSection:number,\n704\t    opts?:GitRunOpts\n705\t):Promise<{ out:Buffer, cut:string[] }>\n706\t```\n707\t\n708\tRules:\n709\t\n710\t- `maxBuffer` must be effectively unlimited for buffered calls (Go has no\n711\t  cap; `ls-files`/`ls-tree` on large repos exceed Node's 1 MiB default).\n712\t  Patch output always goes through `gitPatch`, never `gitOut`.\n713\t- `gitOk` replaces Go's `git(...)` helper (`rev-parse --verify --quiet`,\n714\t  `merge-base` probe).\n715\t- Abort: on `signal` abort, the child is killed and the promise rejects\n716\t  with the signal's reason (an `AbortError`). Every best-effort `catch` in\n717\t  this area (linguist, compactRenames, untracked read, repo head line,\n718\t  temp dir cleanup) must rethrow when `signal?.aborted` is true, so\n719\t  cancellation is never mistaken for \"no excludes\" or \"no renames\".\n720\t- Every `diff` argument list is built with `DIFF_CONFIG` first (2.1).\n721\t- `check-attr` stdin uses `input`; the extra env for it is\n722\t  `{ GIT_ATTR_NOSYSTEM: '1', ...pinnedEnv }` and the args start with\n723\t  `-c core.attributesFile=/dev/null`.\n724\t- Temp index dir: `fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(),\n725\t  'roast-scope-idx-'))`, removed with `fs.rm(dir, { recursive: true,\n726\t  force: true })` in `finally`.\n727\t- Debug: namespace `roast:scope` may log each git argv and exit code;\n728\t  never stdout content, file contents, or environment values.\n729\t\n730\t### 3.4 Bytes vs strings\n731\t\n732\tGo strings are byte strings; JS strings are UTF-16. To keep every size\n733\trule byte-exact:\n734\t\n735\t- `SectionCapper` works on `Buffer` chunks, finds `\\n` and the\n736\t  `diff --git ` prefix on bytes, counts `used` in bytes, and collects\n737\t  kept slices; `finish()` returns one `Buffer` decoded once with UTF-8\n738\t  (`buf.toString('utf8')`, lossy) only for `Scope.diff`. `gitPatch`\n739\t  returns the capped `Buffer`, and `diffBytes` keeps those exact bytes for\n740\t  hashing and recording. A section cut mid-sequence therefore ends in\n741\t  U+FFFD in `diff`, before the marker newline, matching what the Go bytes\n742\t  become once JSON-encoded for a provider.\n743\t- `compactRenames` and `splitSections` operate on `Buffer` (searching the\n744\t  byte pattern `\\ndiff --git `); only a section's header and `rename`\n745\t  lines are decoded for `classify` / `renameOf`.\n746\t- `synthesizeNewFileDiff` reads into a `Buffer` (`fs.open` +\n747\t  `read` up to `maxSection + 1`), splits on byte `0x0a`, measures header,\n748\t  body and line lengths in bytes (header via `Buffer.byteLength`), and\n749\t  decodes the final block once.\n750\t- `fit` measures `diffBytes.length`, truncates `diffBytes` on a byte\n751\t  offset (`subarray(0, maxBytes)` plus the marker bytes), and decodes the\n752\t  result for `diff`. `fileDiffBytes` returns byte lengths.\n753\t- Git name output is read as `Buffer` and NUL-split; `decodePaths(buf)`\n754\t  returns `{ paths:string[], skipped:number }` using a fatal UTF-8\n755\t  decoder, so non-UTF-8 names are skipped with a warning note (2.15).\n756\t- `compareBytes(a, b)` = code-point order (e.g. compare\n757\t  `Buffer.from(a)` with `Buffer.compare`); used for every sort.\n758\t\n759\t### 3.5 Survey helpers (repo scope)\n760\t\n761\t- `manifestQuote(name:string):{ text:string, quoted:boolean }` with\n762\t  printable test `/^[\\p{L}\\p{M}\\p{N}\\p{P}\\p{S} ]$/u` per code point.\n763\t  Names are already valid UTF-8 (`decodePaths`), so it takes a string.\n764\t- There is no `cUnquote`: it served only branch scope, and `-z` removes\n765\t  C-quoted name lists.\n766\t\n767\t## 4. Deliberate changes\n768\t\n769\t- Cancellation: `build` and `gitRoot` take an `AbortSignal` (Go's scope\n770\t  package had no context). Aborting kills the running git child and\n771\t  rejects with an `AbortError`; best-effort steps do not swallow it.\n772\t- Windows is unsupported, so `/dev/null` is used literally for\n773\t  `core.attributesFile` (Go used `os.DevNull`).\n774\t- `fit` returns a new Scope instead of mutating in place (no observable\n775\t  difference: the CLI already used the pre-fit diff for grounding).\n776\t- Branch survey scope is not ported (deliberate change 23): no `branch`\n777\t  scope, `includeWorktree`, `baseOID`, `mergeBaseOID`, `cUnquote`, or\n778\t  branch tests.\n779\t- Name lists and `ls-files --others` use `-z` (deliberate change 15).\n780\t- Names that are not valid UTF-8 are skipped with a warning note\n781\t  (deliberate change 24); git output is read as `Buffer`, and `Scope`\n782\t  carries `diffBytes` so hashed or recorded diff bytes are exact.\n783\t- The `fit` breakdown uses a stable sort (deliberate change 18).\n784\t- Staged linguist exclusion lists a rename's deleted side too, via\n785\t  `--no-renames` (deliberate change 16).\n786\t- `diff.orderFile` is overridden on every `diff` invocation with\n787\t  `DIFF_CONFIG` (deliberate change 17); verify the empty value against\n788\t  git during implementation.\n789\t- Go `unicode.IsPrint` vs JS `\\p{...}` differences for newly assigned\n790\t  code points in `manifestQuote` are accepted.\n791\t\n792\tNothing else changes: other argument lists, note strings, manifest layout, and\n793\terror messages are as in Go.\n794\t\n795\t## 5. Test plan\n796\t\n797\tSeam: scope tests call `build()` (and `fit`, `gitRoot`) against real\n798\ttemporary Git repositories created with `git init` plus\n799\t`user.email`/`user.name` config, because `Notes`, `Manifest`, OIDs and\n800\tcut lists are only partly observable through the CLI. Assertions use\n801\tstructure (file lists, presence of a path in a diff header, counts of\n802\t`SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER` occurrences, `+`/`-` line counts), not note\n803\twording; a note \"names a file\" check is a path substring check. A few\n804\tCLI-level tests (in-process `run`, fake inference server, approved\n805\tconfig) pin the flag wiring by asserting on `meta.json` `scope.files`\n806\tand on the diff text the fake server received.\n807\t\n808\tTests that set `GIT_INDEX_FILE` or other git env must restore it.\n809\tEvery test repo lives in a temp dir; no test runs git in this checkout.\n810\t\n811\t**Pathspecs and excludes**\n812\t\n813\t- Excludes filter both the diff text and `files`\n814\t  (TestExcludesFilterBothDiffAndFiles).\n815\t- Untracked synthesis honors `--paths` and excludes\n816\t  (TestUntrackedSynthesisHonorsExcludesAndPaths).\n817\t- Glob matcher cases from 2.14 (TestGlobMatch) -- pure function test.\n818\t- Default scope with `scope` unset equals explicit `diff`\n819\t  (TestDiffScopeUnchangedByDefaultAndExplicit).\n820\t- Unknown scope string (including `branch`) is an error\n821\t  (TestBuildUnknownScopeErrors).\n822\t- A path with non-ASCII characters appears unquoted in `files` for tracked\n823\t  and untracked changes, and the untracked file is synthesized (new,\n824\t  replaces the Go quoted-path skip).\n825\t- A file name that is not valid UTF-8 is skipped with a warning note and\n826\t  absent from `files`; the build still succeeds. Create the name with a\n827\t  `Buffer` path; skip the test on filesystems that refuse such names\n828\t  (macOS APFS).\n829\t\n830\t**Renames**\n831\t\n832\t- A move is a delete+add under `--no-renames`, with excludes applied to\n833\t  each side (TestRenameBecomesDeleteAdd).\n834\t- Moved files with small edits collapse to rename sections with only the\n835\t  edited lines (TestMovedFilesCollapseToRenameSections).\n836\t- A pure move has a rename header and no hunks (TestPureMoveHasNoHunks).\n837\t- A move across an exclude boundary is not paired; the excluded side\n838\t  never appears (TestMovesAcrossExcludesAreNotPaired).\n839\t- An uncommitted (staged) move collapses too (TestUncommittedMoveCollapses).\n840\t- `fit` breakdown counts a collapsed move for both paths\n841\t  (TestFitBreakdownCountsCollapsedMoves).\n842\t- Record walk does not misread a path starting with `R` as a status\n843\t  (TestMoveCollapsesAfterPathStartingWithR).\n844\t\n845\t**Staged mode**\n846\t\n847\t- Staged reads only the index; worktree-only edits are absent\n848\t  (TestStagedModeReadsIndexOnly).\n849\t- An alternate `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (as in `git commit <paths>`) is honored\n850\t  and the shared index's staged content does not leak\n851\t  (TestStagedModeHonorsGitIndexFile).\n852\t\n853\t**Base and empty repo**\n854\t\n855\t- No commits: diff against the empty tree, staged + untracked reviewed,\n856\t  note present (TestEmptyRepoEmptyTreeFallback).\n857\t- Base fallback notes: `using base` when not `origin/main`, `no usable\n858\t  base` when none resolves (TestBaseFallbackNotes).\n859\t\n860\t**Size caps**\n861\t\n862\t- Two 24 MB files (one committed, one untracked) with a 64 KiB cap: diff\n863\t  stays under 4x the cap, two omission markers, a small later file is\n864\t  intact, notes name both cut files, all three in `files`\n865\t  (TestBuildBoundsHugeFileDiffs). The Go allocated-bytes\n866\t  assertion becomes an RSS bound: peak `process.memoryUsage().rss` growth\n867\t  stays well below the file size (spec.md, Testing Decisions).\n868\t- Capper keeps small sections byte-identical, cuts only the large one,\n869\t  reports its name, and places the marker on its own line; with a huge\n870\t  cap output equals input (TestCapSectionsKeepsSmallSectionsWhole) --\n871\t  pure `SectionCapper` test.\n872\t- `fit` truncation and descending breakdown\n873\t  (TestFitTruncationAndBreakdown); equal-size files keep `files` order\n874\t  (new, deliberate change 18).\n875\t\n876\t**Hardening**\n877\t\n878\t- A committed `.gitattributes` routing `*.go` to a named diff driver,\n879\t  plus hostile global Git config defining that driver's textconv and an\n880\t  external diff (each writing a marker file) and `diff.noprefix` /\n881\t  `diff.mnemonicPrefix`, never runs either driver, for both\n882\t  `base` and `uncommittedOnly` builds (TestBuildIgnoresUserDiffDrivers).\n883\t  Isolate global config via `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` or `HOME`.\n884\t- A global `diff.orderFile` that reverses the order does not change\n885\t  section order, for staged, committed, and uncommitted builds (new,\n886\t  deliberate change 17; also proves the empty-value override works).\n887\t- Untracked secret-denylisted files are skipped and noted\n888\t  (TestBuildSkipsDenylistedUntrackedFiles).\n889\t- Untracked symlinks are not synthesized\n890\t  (TestUntrackedSymlinkNotSynthesized).\n891\t- Repo manifest quotes hostile names (newline, `<`, `>`) and adds the\n892\t  explanation line (TestSurveyManifestQuotesHostileNames).\n893\t\n894\t**Linguist**\n895\t\n896\t- Marked paths are found; `=false` un-marks (TestLinguistIgnores).\n897\t- No `.gitattributes` -> no excludes (TestLinguistIgnoresNoAttributes).\n898\t- Generated files are excluded from diff and files\n899\t  (TestLinguistGeneratedExcludedFromDiff).\n900\t- A file named with glob metacharacters excludes only itself\n901\t  (TestLinguistLiteralPathspec).\n902\t- Staged mode reads attributes from the index, ignoring an unstaged\n903\t  `.gitattributes` edit (TestLinguistStagedUsesIndexAttributes).\n904\t- Exclusions honor the pinned tree, not a later-staged `.gitattributes`\n905\t  (TestLinguistExclusionsHonorThePinnedTree).\n906\t- A staged deletion of a generated file is excluded\n907\t  (TestLinguistStagedDeletionExcluded).\n908\t- A staged rename of a marked file excludes the old path too (new,\n909\t  deliberate change 16).\n910\t- A global `core.attributesFile` marking `*` cannot blank a review\n911\t  (TestLinguistIgnoresGlobalAttributes); isolate `HOME` /\n912\t  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` / `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` in this test.\n913\t- Untracked marked files are excluded\n914\t  (TestLinguistGeneratedExcludesUntracked).\n915\t\n916\t**Repo survey**\n917\t\n918\t- Manifest lists tracked files, directory counts, HEAD line\n919\t  (TestSurveyRepoManifest).\n920\t- Excludes apply to the listing (TestSurveyRepoExcludesApplied).\n921\t- No tracked files -> `isEmpty` (TestSurveyRepoEmptyWhenNoTrackedFiles).\n922\t- Files reflect `headOID`, not the index\n923\t  (TestSurveyRepoFilesReflectHeadOIDNotIndex).\n924\t- Dirty tree drops linguist excludes rather than drifting\n925\t  (TestSurveyRepoDirtyTreeDoesNotDriftLinguistExcludes).\n926\t- A bare directory `--paths` value selects its subtree\n927\t  (TestSurveyRepoDirectoryPathspecMatchesSubtree).\n928\t\n929\t**New in TS**\n930\t\n931\t- Aborting the signal during a large `gitPatch` kills git and rejects\n932\t  with an `AbortError`, also when abort lands inside a best-effort step.\n933\t- `diffBytes` equals the bytes git emitted for sections under the cap\n934\t  (byte-exact for non-UTF-8 content), while `diff` decodes with U+FFFD.\n935\t- Sorting uses code-point order (a path with an astral character sorts\n936\t  after one with U+FFFD).\n937\t\n938\t## 6. Go baseline references\n939\t\n940\tAt commit `cd08f10`:\n941\t\n942\t- `internal/scope/scope.go` -- Build, survey scopes (branch not ported),\n943\t  linguist, globs,\n944\t  untracked synthesis, Fit, GitRoot.\n945\t- `internal/scope/patch.go` -- capSections, sectionName, truncationNote,\n946\t  DefaultMaxFileDiffBytes, sectionOmittedMarker.\n947\t- `internal/scope/renames.go` -- compactRenames, splitSections, classify,\n948\t  renameOf.\n949\t- `internal/scope/scope_test.go`, `linguist_test.go`,\n950\t  `hardening_test.go`.\n951\t- `internal/cli/cli.go` (flag definitions, `--scope` validation,\n952\t  BuildOpts wiring, Fit call, run-record scope meta).\n953\t- `internal/context/tree.go` (`PinIndexTree`).\n954\t- `internal/snapshot/snapshot.go` (`DefaultDenylist`, `Denylisted`).\n955\t\n956\t## 7. Resolved\n957\t\n958\tOpen questions from the draft, resolved by the maintainer (spec.md,\n959\t\"Deliberate changes from the Go baseline\"):\n960\t\n961\t1. Branch survey scope is not ported (deliberate change 23).\n962\t2. Diff name lists and `ls-files --others` use `-z` (deliberate change 15).\n963\t3. Non-UTF-8 file names are skipped with a warning (deliberate change 24);\n964\t   git output is read as `Buffer` (cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\").\n965\t4. The fit breakdown uses a stable sort (deliberate change 18).\n966\t5. Staged generated-file exclusion lists renames' deleted side too\n967\t   (deliberate change 16).\n968\t6. The user's `diff.orderFile` is overridden (deliberate change 17);\n969\t   verify the mechanism against git during implementation.\n970\t7. The huge-file test asserts an RSS bound (spec.md, Testing Decisions).\n971\t8. Go/Node Unicode printable differences are accepted.\n972\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4","uuid":"04417aed-efd6-4c8d-a14e-5436e49683cf","timestamp":"2026-11-18T19:23:58.619Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/nick/.claude/projects/-Users-nick-code-roast-ts-rewrite-10-review-scope/4913b683-6028-48ab-bf68-aa225d725bd4/tool-results/bph59j63h.txt","content":"# Area 04: Review scope\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\nThe scope area decides exactly what a review looks at. Given the Git\ntoplevel and the user's scoping choices (`--staged`, `--base`,\n`--uncommitted-only`, `--paths`, `--exclude`, `--include-vendored`,\n`--scope`), it runs `git` and produces a **Scope**: the diff text the\n**finder** reads (or, for repo scope, a small manifest), the sorted list of\nfiles in scope, the resolved base, the commit/tree identities the rest of\nthe review pins its reads to, and human-facing notes that explain every\nscoping decision.\n\nTwo invariants drive the design:\n\n1. The file list is derived from the same pathspec-filtered git queries\n   that produced the diff text. A file outside the post-exclude diff can\n   never reach the finder, the **verifier**, or the report.\n2. Nothing a repository or the user's Git config can do may run a program\n   on, rewrite, or widen the reviewed content: no external diff drivers,\n   no textconv, no global/system attribute files narrowing scope, no\n   following symlinks out of the tree, and secret-looking untracked files\n   are never sent.\n\nDownstream consumers: the CLI `review` command (prints notes, decides\n\"no diff\", records scope into the **run record**), the context area\n(grounding and file readers pin to `staged` / `stagedTreeOID` /\n`headOID`), and the finder prompt (diff or manifest). The pre-commit\n**gate** reaches this area through `review --staged`.\n\n## 2. Behavior contract\n\nAll byte counts are UTF-8 byte counts. All git commands below are run as `git\n-C <repo> <args...>` with the parent environment inherited (so an ambient\n`GIT_INDEX_FILE`, `GIT_DIR`, etc. is honored, see 2.6), stdout captured as a\n`Buffer`, and stderr captured for error messages. `<repo>` is always the Git\ntoplevel (from `gitRoot`, 2.13).\n\n### 2.1 Constants\n\n`DEFAULT_IGNORES` (order matters for argument order):\n\n```\nvendor/**\nnode_modules/**\n**/node_modules/**\n*.lock\npackage-lock.json\nyarn.lock\npnpm-lock.yaml\ngo.sum\n*.pb.go\n*_pb2.py\n*_pb2_grpc.py\n*.min.js\n*.min.css\n```\n\n`PATCH_FLAGS` go on every git invocation that produces a patch or a name\nlist from `diff` (so a repo's `.gitattributes` diff driver, the user's\n`diff.external`, color, and prefix settings cannot change the text):\n\n```\n--no-ext-diff --no-textconv --no-color --src-prefix=a/ --dst-prefix=b/\n```\n\n`DIFF_FLAGS` = `--no-renames` followed by `PATCH_FLAGS`. Every diff-mode\n`git diff` uses `DIFF_FLAGS`. Renames are recovered later (2.8) only for\npairs whose both sides survived the excludes.\n\n`DIFF_CONFIG` = `-c diff.orderFile= -c core.quotePath=false`, placed\nbefore the subcommand. Every\ngit invocation whose subcommand is `diff` in this file, and in the context\narea's own `git diff` calls, runs as `git -C <repo> DIFF_CONFIG diff ...`\n(the argument lists below omit it for brevity). It overrides the user's\n`diff.orderFile` so section order is always git's default path order\n(deliberate change 17, spec.md). The empty value must be verified against\nthe installed git during implementation (a test with a global\n`diff.orderFile` that reverses the order, 5). If git rejects an empty\nvalue or still applies the file, use `-c diff.orderFile=/dev/null`\ninstead; the contract is only that the user's order file has no effect.\n`core.quotePath=false` keeps non-ASCII paths unquoted in patch headers\n(`diff --git a/... b/...`), so the fit breakdown attributes their bytes\nto the right file (deliberate change 15); git still quotes paths with\ntabs, newlines, or double quotes, and those report 0 bytes in the\nbreakdown as in Go.\n\n`BASE_CANDIDATES`, tried in order when no `--base` is given (diff scope\nonly): `origin/main`, `origin/master`, `main`, `master`, `HEAD~1`.\n\n`EMPTY_TREE` = `4b825dc642cb6eb9a060e54bf8d69288fbee4904` (git's empty\ntree; lets a repo with no commits be diffed).\n\n`DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES` = `1_000_000`.\n\n`SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(n)` is exactly this single line (with `n` in\ndecimal, ending in a newline; it exceeds 80 columns by necessity):\n\n```\n\\ roast: the rest of this file's diff is omitted (over <n> bytes); use read_file to inspect the file\n```\n\nThe leading `\\` makes it diff metadata (like git's `\\ No newline at end\nof file`), so the grounder and rename parser skip it as content.\n\n`LINGUIST_ATTRS` = `linguist-generated`, `linguist-vendored`.\n\n### 2.2 Output: the Scope value\n\n- `diff` -- combined diff text: `diffBytes` decoded once as UTF-8 with\n  U+FFFD replacement, for models and reports. Empty in repo scope.\n- `files` -- sorted (bytewise, 2.15) unique repo-relative paths in scope.\n- `base` -- resolved base ref *name* (e.g. `origin/main`), not an OID.\n  Empty for uncommitted-only, staged, no-usable-base, and repo scope.\n- `diffBytes` -- the same combined diff as a `Buffer`: the exact bytes git\n  and the untracked reader produced (after capping), so anything hashed or\n  recorded is byte-exact (cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\", spec.md).\n- `notes` -- human-facing scoping notes, in the order generated. The CLI\n  prints each to stderr.\n- `staged` -- true only for `stagedOnly`; file context must come from the\n  index (or the pinned tree), not the worktree.\n- `stagedTreeOID` -- echo of `BuildOpts.stagedTreeOID` in staged mode;\n  else empty.\n- `manifest` -- survey map (repo scope); empty in diff scope.\n- `survey` -- true for `repo` scope.\n- `headOID` -- repo scope: `rev-parse HEAD`; empty if no commits.\n  Empty in diff scope.\n\n`isEmpty(scope)`: in repo scope, true when `files` is empty; otherwise\ntrue when `diff` is empty after trimming whitespace. The CLI then writes\na report with assessment `NO_DIFF` and exits 0 (CLI area).\n\n### 2.3 Inputs: BuildOpts and how the CLI fills them\n\n- `base` -- `--base` (string, default `\"\"`).\n- `uncommittedOnly` -- `--uncommitted-only`.\n- `stagedOnly` -- `--staged`.\n- `paths` -- `--paths` (string slice: comma-separated and repeatable).\n- `excludes` -- `.roast.json` `review.excludes`, followed by `--exclude`\n  values.\n- `includeVendored` -- `--include-vendored`.\n- `scope` -- `--scope` (`diff` default; the CLI accepts only `diff` or\n  `repo`).\n- `stagedTreeOID` -- with `--staged`, the OID from the context area's\n  `pinIndexTree(repo)`, computed before `build`.\n- `maxFileDiffBytes` -- the whole-diff budget: `limits.max_diff_bytes`,\n  else 1_000_000, or 200_000 with `--quick`.\n\n`maxFileDiffBytes <= 0` (or absent) means `DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES`.\nBelow this, `maxSection` names the effective value.\n\nCLI-level validation (owned by the CLI area, restated for context):\n`--scope` other than `diff`/`repo` is a usage error; `--scope repo` with\n`--quick` is a usage error; `--scope repo` with any of `--staged`,\n`--uncommitted-only`, `--base` is a usage error. `build` itself accepts\n`\"\"`, `diff`, `repo` and rejects anything else with\n`unsupported scope \"<x>\": expected \"\", \"diff\", or \"repo\"`.\n`--staged` together with `--uncommitted-only` or `--base` is not rejected;\nstaged mode simply ignores both.\n\n### 2.4 Common prelude (all scopes)\n\n1. `allExcludes` = `opts.excludes`, then (unless `includeVendored`)\n   `DEFAULT_IGNORES` appended.\n2. Unless `includeVendored`: `linguistExcludes = linguistIgnores(repo,\n   cached = stagedOnly, treeOID = stagedTreeOID)` (2.7). If non-empty,\n   note `excluding <N> path(s) marked linguist-generated/-vendored in\n   .gitattributes (--include-vendored to review)`.\n3. `spec = pathspecArgs(paths, allExcludes, linguistExcludes)`:\n   - if all three lists are empty: no arguments at all;\n   - else `--`, then each path verbatim (git pathspec, user magic allowed),\n     then `:(exclude)<pattern>` per `allExcludes` entry, then\n     `:(exclude,literal)<path>` per `linguistExcludes` entry.\n4. Dispatch on `scope`: `repo` -> 2.12, `\"\"`/`diff` -> 2.5, anything else\n   -> the `unsupported scope` error.\n\nThe linguist step runs before dispatch, so it also runs (uncached) for\nrepo scope.\n\n### 2.5 Diff scope\n\n**No-commit detection.** `head = \"HEAD\"`. Run `rev-parse --verify --quiet\nHEAD` (stdout/stderr discarded). On non-zero exit: `head = EMPTY_TREE`,\nforce `uncommittedOnly = true`, note `repository has no commits;\nreviewing staged + untracked files`.\n\n**Staged mode** (`stagedOnly`), what a commit would contain:\n\n1. `stagedRef` = `[\"--cached\", head]`, or `[head, stagedTreeOID]` when\n   `stagedTreeOID` is set (the pinned tree replaces the live index; same\n   comparison, immutable OID).\n2. Patch: `diff DIFF_FLAGS stagedRef spec` through the section capper\n   (2.9). Failure -> error `git diff (staged): <cause>`.\n3. `notes = truncationNote(notes, cut, maxSection)`.\n4. `diff, n = compactRenames(stagedRef, spec, diff)`; `renameNote(n)`.\n5. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z stagedRef spec`. Failure ->\n   error `git diff --name-only (staged): <cause>`. `files` = the decoded\n   paths (NUL-split, 2.15), sorted.\n6. Return `{diff, files, notes, staged:true, stagedTreeOID}`; `base`\n   empty. No untracked files, no worktree content.\n\n**Default mode** (committed-since-base + uncommitted + untracked):\n\n1. Unless `uncommittedOnly`:\n   1. `resolvedBase = resolveBase(base)`: candidates are `[base]` if given,\n      else `BASE_CANDIDATES`; the first for which `rev-parse --verify\n      --quiet <ref>` exits 0 wins; none -> `\"\"`.\n   2. If `\"\"`: note `no usable base ref (tried <T>); reviewing uncommitted\n      changes only` where `<T>` is the requested base, or\n      `BASE_CANDIDATES.join(\", \")`. Skip to step 2.\n   3. If no base was requested and `resolvedBase !== \"origin/main\"`: note\n      `using base '<resolvedBase>'`.\n   4. `rangeArgs = buildRangeArgs(resolvedBase)`: run `merge-base <base>\n      HEAD` (output discarded); exit 0 -> `[\"<base>...HEAD\"]`, else\n      `[\"<base>\", \"HEAD\"]`.\n   5. Patch: `diff DIFF_FLAGS rangeArgs spec` through the capper. Failure ->\n      `git diff (committed): <cause>`. Cut names go to `cutSections`.\n   6. `committed, n = compactRenames(rangeArgs, spec, committed)`;\n      `renameNote(n)`.\n   7. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z rangeArgs spec`. Failure ->\n      `git diff --name-only (committed): <cause>`.\n2. Uncommitted (worktree and index vs `head`): patch `diff DIFF_FLAGS\n   <head> spec` through the capper; failure -> `git diff (uncommitted):\n   <cause>`; cut names appended to `cutSections`; `compactRenames([head],\n   spec, ...)`; `renameNote`. Names: `diff DIFF_FLAGS --name-only -z\n   <head> spec`; failure -> `git diff --name-only (uncommitted): <cause>`.\n3. `diff = committed`; if `uncommitted.trim() !== \"\"`, append\n   `\"\\n\\n### Uncommitted changes ###\\n\" + uncommitted` (the separator is\n   added even when `committed` is empty).\n4. `fileSet` = the decoded paths of both name lists (2.15).\n5. Untracked files (2.10) are appended to `diff` and `fileSet`.\n6. `notes = truncationNote(notes, cutSections, maxSection)` -- one note for\n   committed, uncommitted, and untracked cuts together, emitted after the\n   untracked denylist note.\n7. Return `{diff, files: sorted fileSet, base: resolvedBase, notes}`.\n\nName lists use `-z` (deliberate change 15): paths arrive NUL-separated and\nverbatim, never C-quoted, so a path with non-ASCII, tab, or quote characters\nis a normal entry in `files`. Patch text still C-quotes such paths in its\n`diff --git` headers (see 2.8, 2.16). Binary files appear as git prints them\nwithout `--binary` or `--text` (`Binary files a/x and b/x differ`); there is\nno other binary handling for tracked files.\n\n### 2.6 Ambient Git environment\n\nGit subprocesses inherit the full parent environment. In particular a hook\ninvoked by `git commit <paths>` sets `GIT_INDEX_FILE` to an alternate\nindex; staged mode must review that index, not `.git/index`. (The pinned\ntree from `pinIndexTree` already honors it; the `--cached` fallback does\ntoo by inheritance.)\n\n### 2.7 Linguist generated/vendored exclusion\n\nA repo's in-tree `.gitattributes` may mark paths `linguist-generated` or\n`linguist-vendored`; those are treated like `DEFAULT_IGNORES` and are\nreviewed only with `--include-vendored`.\n\n**`linguistIgnores(repo, cached, treeOID)`**, best-effort (every failure\nreturns no excludes, so the review proceeds unfiltered -- never narrower):\n\n1. Pinned index: only when `cached && treeOID`: create a temp dir\n   (`<tmpdir>/roast-scope-idx-*`), `idx = <dir>/index`, extra env\n   `GIT_INDEX_FILE=<idx>`, run `read-tree <treeOID>` with that env.\n   Failure -> remove the dir, return no excludes (do NOT fall back to the\n   live index). The temp dir is removed when the function returns.\n2. `ls-files -z` (with the extra env) -> NUL-split paths, decoded per 2.15\n   (invalid names skipped, counted). Failure -> none.\n3. Only when `cached`: also `diff DIFF_FLAGS --cached --name-only -z`\n   (with the extra env; `DIFF_FLAGS` includes `--no-renames`, deliberate\n   change 16); on success append its paths. With renames off, a staged\n   rename lists both its old and new path, so a staged deletion or rename\n   source of a marked file, absent from the index, is still excluded.\n   Failure here is ignored.\n4. No paths -> none. Else `linguistMarked(paths, cached, env)`, sorted.\n\n**`linguistMarked(repo, paths, cached, env)`**:\n\n- Empty `paths` -> none.\n- Command: `git -C <repo> -c core.attributesFile=/dev/null check-attr\n  --stdin -z [--cached] linguist-generated linguist-vendored`.\n- Environment: parent env, plus `GIT_ATTR_NOSYSTEM=1`, plus `env` last\n  (so a pinned `GIT_INDEX_FILE` wins). These two drop the user-global and\n  system attribute files; only in-tree/index attributes (and\n  `$GIT_DIR/info/attributes`, which cannot be disabled) can narrow scope.\n- Stdin: `paths.join(\"\\0\") + \"\\0\"`.\n- Any failure -> none.\n- Output is NUL-separated `(path, attr, value)` triples; a short trailing\n  group is ignored. A path is marked when `value` is `set` or `true`\n  (`unspecified`, `unset`, `false` do not count, so\n  `dir/x linguist-generated=false` un-marks). Deduplicate, keeping first\n  occurrence order.\n\n**Attribute source per mode**: staged -> index (`--cached`), pinned to\n`stagedTreeOID` when set; every other mode -> worktree `.gitattributes`.\nUntracked candidates always use the worktree (`cached = false`, no pinned\nenv), regardless of mode.\n\nLinguist excludes are exact paths and go into pathspecs with\n`:(exclude,literal)` magic, so a file literally named `*.go` excludes only\nitself.\n\n### 2.8 Rename compaction\n\nDiffs use `--no-renames` so excludes apply to both sides of a move.\n`compactRenames(repo, ref, spec, diffText, maxSection)` then collapses\nmoves whose two sides both survived the excludes. It returns\n`(diffText', pairsCollapsed)` and on any git error returns the input\nunchanged with 0.\n\n**Sections.** `splitSections(text)`: starting at offset 0, each section\nruns up to and including the `\\n` before the next occurrence of\n`\"\\ndiff --git \"` searched from `start + 1`; the last runs to the end.\nLeading text before the first header is its own section.\n`classify(section)`:\n\n- Header = first line. It must start with `diff --git `; the remainder\n  `pair` must not start with `\"`, must have `len >= 7` and\n  `(len - 5)` even; with `n = (len - 5) / 2`, `path = pair[2 .. 2+n)`,\n  require `pair[0..2) == \"a/\"` and `pair[2+n..) == \" b/\" + path`.\n  Otherwise kind `other`.\n- Scan the following lines: the first of `@@...` or `--- ...` -> `other`;\n  `deleted file mode ...` -> `deleted`; `new file mode ...` -> `added`.\n\n**Algorithm.**\n\n1. Collect `deleted` and `added` paths. Either empty -> unchanged, 0.\n2. `diff -M PATCH_FLAGS --name-status -z <ref...> <spec...>` (failure ->\n   unchanged). Walk NUL fields as records: a status field starting with\n   `R` or `C` takes two paths (`from`, `to`), any other status one path\n   (advance 2). A truncated R/C record stops the walk. Accept `(from, to)`\n   only when status starts with `R`, `from` is a deleted path and `to` is\n   an added path of this diff. (Walking whole records is required: a path\n   like `README.md` must never be read as a status.)\n3. No accepted pairs -> unchanged.\n4. `diff -M PATCH_FLAGS <ref...> <spec...>` through the section capper with\n   the same `maxSection` (its cut list is discarded; failure ->\n   unchanged).\n5. For each section of that output, `renameOf(section)`: scan lines until\n   one starts with `@@`; take values of `rename from ` and `rename to `\n   lines; reject when either is empty or starts with `\"` (quoted paths are\n   left uncollapsed). Keep the section when `(from, to)` is accepted; mark\n   both paths `paired`; append the section text, plus `\\n` if it does not\n   end in one.\n6. No paired paths -> unchanged.\n7. Output: every original section in order except `deleted`/`added`\n   sections whose path is paired; then a `\\n` if the output is non-empty\n   and does not end in one; then all kept rename sections. Count =\n   `paired.size / 2`.\n\n`renameNote(n)` for `n > 0`: `collapsed <n> moved file(s) to git's rename\nform (both paths in scope)`. A pure move therefore has a rename header and\nno hunks; a move with a small edit shows only the edited lines.\n\n### 2.9 Patch production and per-file cap\n\n`gitPatch(repo, args, maxSection)` runs git and streams stdout through a\nsection capper, so memory per file is bounded by `maxSection` (a 24 MB\nfile must not be buffered whole). Algorithm on the byte stream, processed\nline by line (a line longer than the read buffer arrives in fragments;\nonly a fragment at line start can be a header):\n\n- State: `header = \"\"`, `used = 0`, `dropping = false`.\n- A fragment at line start beginning with `diff --git ` first finishes the\n  previous section, then sets `header` to that line (without newline),\n  `used = 0`, `dropping = false`.\n- While not dropping, keep `take = min(len, maxSection - used)` bytes of\n  the fragment; if `take < len`, start dropping.\n- Finishing a section that was dropping: if the output does not end in\n  `\\n`, add one; append `SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(maxSection)`; push\n  `sectionName(header)` to `cut`.\n- At EOF, finish the last section.\n- Text before the first header counts toward a section named `\"\"`.\n- Non-zero git exit -> error `exit status <code>: <trimmed stderr>`; a\n  read error kills git and reports the read error.\n\n`sectionName(header)`: strip `diff --git `; if it is `a/P b/P` (same\nlength rule as `classify`), return `P`; else the raw remainder.\n\n`truncationNote(cut, maxSection)` for non-empty `cut`: `diff for <N>\nfile(s) cut at <maxSection> bytes each: <listSome(cut, 10)>`.\n`listSome(items, 10)`: up to 10 joined by `, `; beyond 10, the first 10\nthen `, and <K> more`.\n\nThe cut is at a byte offset and may split a multi-byte UTF-8 sequence;\nsee 3.4 for decoding.\n\n### 2.10 Untracked files (default diff mode only)\n\n1. `ls-files --others --exclude-standard -z` (deliberate change 15);\n   failure -> error `git ls-files --others: <cause>`.\n2. For each decoded path `u` (2.15; names that are not valid UTF-8 are\n   skipped and counted), in git's order:\n   - skip unless `untrackedPasses(u, paths, allExcludes)` (2.14);\n   - if `snapshot.denylisted(u)` -> add to `denied` (the snapshot area's\n     secret denylist; applies even with `--include-vendored`);\n   - else candidate.\n3. `denied` non-empty -> note `skipped <N> untracked file(s) matching the\n   secret denylist: <listSome(denied, 10)>`.\n4. Unless `includeVendored`, drop candidates returned by\n   `linguistMarked(repo, candidates, cached=false, env=none)`.\n5. For each remaining candidate, `synthesizeNewFileDiff(u, maxSection)`;\n   empty result -> skipped silently (not added to `files`); cut -> add `u`\n   to `cutSections`; else add the block and add `u` to `fileSet`.\n6. If any blocks: if `diff.trim() !== \"\"` append `\"\\n\\n### Untracked files\n   (treated as new-file additions) ###\\n\"`; then append\n   `blocks.join(\"\\n\")`.\n\n**`synthesizeNewFileDiff(relpath, maxSection)`**:\n\n- `lstat(<repo>/<relpath>)`; not a regular file (symlink, dir, missing,\n  error) -> `\"\"`. Symlinks are never followed.\n- Read at most `maxSection + 1` bytes; any error -> `\"\"`. `cut =\n  bytesRead > maxSection`.\n- Split on `\\n`; drop one trailing empty element.\n- `header = \"diff --git a/P b/P\\nnew file mode 100644\\n--- /dev/null\\n+++\n  b/P\\n\"` with `P = relpath` verbatim (no quoting).\n- For each line: if `len(header) + len(body) + len(line) + 2 >\n  maxSection`, set `cut` and stop; else append `\"+\" + line + \"\\n\"` and\n  count it.\n- Result: header, `@@ -0,0 +1,<count> @@\\n`, body, and if `cut`\n  `SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER(maxSection)`.\n- No binary detection: content is treated as text bytes. An empty file\n  yields `@@ -0,0 +1,0 @@` and no body.\n\n### 2.11 Branch survey scope (not ported)\n\nThe Go `branch` scope was unreachable from the CLI and is intentionally\nabsent from the TypeScript port (deliberate change 23, spec.md); this\nnumber is kept so later references stay stable.\n\n### 2.12 Repo survey scope (`scope: \"repo\"`)\n\nReviews the committed tree at HEAD as one snapshot.\n\n1. `status --porcelain` (failure -> `git status --porcelain: <cause>`).\n   If non-blank: if `linguistExcludes` non-empty, note `working tree is\n   dirty: skipping .gitattributes-based excludes for repo scope (they were\n   resolved from the mutable index/worktree, not HeadOID)`; set\n   `linguistExcludes = []`; then note `working tree has uncommitted\n   changes not reflected in this repo-scope snapshot (pinned to\n   HeadOID)`.\n2. If `rev-parse --verify --quiet HEAD` succeeds: `headOID = rev-parse\n   HEAD` trimmed (failure -> `git rev-parse HEAD: <cause>`). Else note\n   `repository has no commits; manifest is empty`.\n3. With a `headOID`: `ls-tree -r --name-only -z <headOID>` (failure ->\n   `git ls-tree: <cause>`). Keep each decoded path (2.15; non-UTF-8 names\n   are skipped and counted) that is not in the\n   `linguistExcludes` set (exact membership, not glob) and that passes\n   `untrackedPasses(path, paths, allExcludes)`. Sort.\n   (Read from the HEAD tree, not the index, so staged changes do not leak\n   into or out of the listing.)\n4. `headLine`: with a `headOID`, `log --oneline --no-color\n   --no-show-signature -1` trimmed; errors ignored (empty).\n5. Top-level directory of each file = text before the first `/`, or the\n   whole name for a root file. Count per directory; sort directory names.\n6. Manifest, with `q(s)` = `manifestQuote(s)` (2.12.1) applied to the\n   head line, directory names, and file names:\n   ```\n   ### Repository snapshot ###\n   HEAD: <q(headLine)>            (or \"HEAD: (no commits)\" when empty)\n   <F> tracked file(s) across <D> top-level director(ies)\n\n   ### Files by top-level directory ###\n   <q(dir)> (<count>)             (one per directory)\n\n   ### Tracked files ###\n   <q(file)>                      (one per file)\n   ```\n   Every line ends in `\\n`. If any `q` call quoted, append\n   `\\n(Names in double quotes contain control or markup characters and\n   are Go-escaped; unescape one before passing it to a tool.)\\n`.\n7. Return `{manifest, survey:true, files, headOID, notes}`; `base`,\n   `baseOID`, `mergeBaseOID` empty.\n\n#### 2.12.1 manifestQuote\n\nA name is safe (returned unchanged) when it contains none\nof `\"`, `\\`, `<`, `>`, and every code point is printable per Go's\n`unicode.IsPrint`: categories L, M, N, P, S, plus U+0020 only (other\nspaces, controls, format characters, line/paragraph separators are not).\nOtherwise return Go `strconv.Quote(s)` with every `<` replaced by `\\x3c`\nand every `>` by `\\x3e`. `strconv.Quote` rules: wrap in `\"`; `\"` and `\\`\nescaped with `\\`; printable code points literal; `\\a \\b \\f \\n \\r \\t \\v`\nfor those controls; other code points below U+0020 and U+007F as `\\xNN`;\nother non-printable code points as\n`\\uXXXX` (< U+10000) or `\\UXXXXXXXX`. Hex digits are lowercase. Names\nreaching it are always valid UTF-8 (2.15), so Go's invalid-byte `\\xNN`\ncase does not arise. Unicode printable differences between Go's\n`unicode.IsPrint` and JS `\\p{...}` classes for newly assigned code points\nare accepted (deliberate change, section 4).\n\n### 2.13 gitRoot\n\n`gitRoot(start)`: `git -C <start> rev-parse --show-toplevel`, trimmed.\nAny failure -> error `not inside a git repository`. Used by every command\nthat needs the repo (review, init, trust, doctor, gate, skill, ignore,\nruns, config discovery).\n\n### 2.14 In-process glob matching (`untrackedPasses`)\n\nUsed for untracked files and the repo-scope listing (git's pathspec\nengine handles tracked diffs).\n\n`globToRegExp(pattern)` builds an anchored full-match regex scanning the\npattern byte by byte:\n\n- `**` -> `.*`, and if immediately followed by `/`, that `/` becomes `/?`;\n- `*` -> `.*` (crosses `/`, Python `fnmatch` semantics);\n- `?` -> `.` (one character);\n- each of `. + ^ $ { } ( ) [ ] |` and `\\` -> escaped literally (so `[...]`\n  classes are NOT supported);\n- anything else literal.\n\n`.` must not match `\\n` and must match every other character (Go RE2\nsemantics; in JS use `[^\\n]` rather than `.`).\n\n`globMatch(path, pattern)`: match `pattern`; if not, and replacing every\n`**` with `*` changes it, match that instead.\n\n`untrackedPasses(path, paths, excludes)`:\n\n1. Any exclude glob matches -> false.\n2. `paths` empty -> true.\n3. For each path pattern: glob match -> true; or, when the pattern\n   contains none of `*`, `?`, `[`, let `dir` = pattern without one\n   trailing `/`; `dir` non-empty and `path` starts with `dir + \"/\"` ->\n   true (a bare directory selects its subtree, as git pathspecs do).\n4. Otherwise false.\n\nExamples: `vendor/foo/bar.go` matches `vendor/**`; `a/node_modules/d.js`\nmatches `**/node_modules/**`; `foo.pb.go` matches `*.pb.go`; `keep.go`\nmatches neither `*.pb.go` nor `vendor/**`.\n\n### 2.15 Ordering and text rules\n\n- All sorts are bytewise over UTF-8 (equivalently by Unicode code point),\n  not JS's default UTF-16 order.\n- \"Non-empty lines\" = split on `\\n`, drop empty strings (no trimming).\n- NUL splitting: split on `\\0`, drop exactly one trailing empty element;\n  empty input -> no elements. It is done on the raw `Buffer`.\n- Path decoding (deliberate change 24): each NUL-split path from\n  `diff --name-only -z`, `ls-files --others -z`, `ls-files -z`, and\n  `ls-tree -z` is decoded with `new TextDecoder('utf-8', { fatal: true })`.\n  A path that is not valid UTF-8 is skipped and counted (it cannot\n  round-trip through a JS string). If the total count for the build is\n  non-zero, the build adds the note `skipped <N> path(s) whose names are\n  not valid UTF-8`, once, as its last note. Git's pathspec filtering has\n  already happened, so such a file's section may still appear in the diff\n  text; only the path is absent from `files`. Records from the rename\n  `--name-status -z` walk (2.8) skip invalid names silently.\n- \"Trim\" = remove leading/trailing whitespace (Go `strings.TrimSpace`).\n\n### 2.16 Fit (whole-diff budget)\n\n`fit(scope, maxBytes)` (called by the CLI after grounding has captured\nthe pre-fit diff):\n\n- `byteLength(diff) <= maxBytes` -> unchanged, no breakdown.\n- Else breakdown: for each file in `files`, `{path, bytes:\n  fileDiffBytes(file)}`, sorted by bytes descending with a stable sort:\n  ties keep `files` order (deliberate change 18; JS `Array.prototype.sort`\n  is stable). New diff = first `maxBytes` bytes + `\"\\n... (diff\n  truncated)\\n\"`; note `diff truncated to <maxBytes> bytes`.\n\n`fileDiffBytes(diff, P)`: starting at `pos = 0`, repeatedly find\n`diff --git a/P b/P` at or after `pos` (index `start`); the block ends at\n`end` = the index of the `\\n` that precedes the next `\"\\ndiff --git \"`\nfound searching from `start + 1`, or the end of the diff; add\n`end - start`; set `pos = end`. Then add the full length of every section\n(2.8 `splitSections`) whose `renameOf` yields `from === P` or `to === P`\n(a collapsed move's header names two paths, so it counts for both).\n\nA path that git C-quotes in `diff --git` headers (non-ASCII, tab, quote)\nnever matches the literal `a/P b/P` search, so its breakdown entry is 0\nbytes; this is accepted.\n\nThe CLI renders the breakdown in the report (render area).\n\n### 2.17 Error surface\n\n`build` errors are plain errors (never usage errors). The CLI prints them\nand maps uncategorized errors to its default operational exit code (CLI\narea). Best-effort steps (linguist, rename compaction, untracked\nsynthesis of one file, repo-scope head line) never fail the build, with\nthe exception of cancellation (3.3).\n\n## 3. TypeScript module design\n\n### 3.1 Files under `src/scope/`\n\n```\nsrc/scope/constants.ts  DEFAULT_IGNORES, PATCH_FLAGS, DIFF_FLAGS,\n                        DIFF_CONFIG, BASE_CANDIDATES, EMPTY_TREE,\n                        DEFAULT_MAX_FILE_DIFF_BYTES, sectionOmittedMarker\nsrc/scope/git.ts        git subprocess helpers (execFile + spawn)\nsrc/scope/patch.ts      SectionCapper, sectionName, truncationNote,\n                        listSome\nsrc/scope/renames.ts    splitSections, classify, renameOf, compactRenames,\n                        renameNote\nsrc/scope/glob.ts       globToRegExp, globMatch, untrackedPasses\nsrc/scope/linguist.ts   linguistIgnores, linguistMarked\nsrc/scope/untracked.ts  synthesizeNewFileDiff\nsrc/scope/survey.ts     buildSurveyRepo, manifestQuote, goQuote\nsrc/scope/build.ts      build, isEmpty, fit, fileDiffBytes, gitRoot,\n                        resolveBase, buildRangeArgs, pathspecArgs\nsrc/scope/text.ts       compareBytes, nonEmptyLines, splitNul,\n                        decodePaths\n```\n\nDepends on `src/snapshot/` (`denylisted(path)`) only. Used by `src/cli/`\n(review, and `gitRoot` everywhere), `src/context/` (imports `PATCH_FLAGS` for\nits own `git diff --no-renames ... <range> -- <file>` calls, prefixed with\n`DIFF_CONFIG`), and indirectly by `src/record/` (the CLI copies scope fields\ninto `meta.json`'s `scope` object).\n\n### 3.2 Types and signatures\n\n```ts\nexport type ScopeKind = ''|'diff'|'repo'\n\nexport interface BuildOpts {\n    base?:string\n    uncommittedOnly?:boolean\n    stagedOnly?:boolean\n    paths?:readonly string[]\n    excludes?:readonly string[]\n    includeVendored?:boolean\n    scope?:string\n    stagedTreeOID?:string\n    maxFileDiffBytes?:number\n}\n\nexport interface Scope {\n    readonly diff:string\n    readonly diffBytes:Buffer\n    readonly files:readonly string[]\n    readonly base:string\n    readonly notes:readonly string[]\n    readonly staged:boolean\n    readonly stagedTreeOID:string\n    readonly manifest:string\n    readonly survey:boolean\n    readonly headOID:string\n}\n\nexport interface SizeEntry {\n    path:string\n    bytes:number\n}\n\nexport function build (\n    repo:string,\n    opts:BuildOpts,\n    signal?:AbortSignal\n):Promise<Scope>\n\nexport function isEmpty (scope:Scope):boolean\n\n// Pure; returns a new Scope rather than mutating (the CLI keeps the\n// pre-fit diff for grounding).\nexport function fit (\n    scope:Scope,\n    maxBytes:number\n):{ scope:Scope, breakdown:SizeEntry[]|null }\n\nexport function gitRoot (\n    start:string,\n    signal?:AbortSignal\n):Promise<string>\n```\n\n### 3.3 git.ts\n\n```ts\nexport class GitError extends Error {\n    // message: `exit status <code>: <trimmed stderr>`\n    exitCode:number|null\n    stderr:string\n}\n\nexport interface GitRunOpts {\n    signal?:AbortSignal\n    env?:Record<string, string>   // merged over process.env\n    input?:Buffer|string          // written to stdin, then closed\n}\n\n// Buffered: execFile('git', ['-C', repo, ...args], {\n//   encoding: 'buffer', maxBuffer: Infinity, signal,\n//   env: { ...process.env, ...opts.env } })\nexport function gitOut (\n    repo:string, args:readonly string[], opts?:GitRunOpts\n):Promise<Buffer>\n\n// Exit status only; stdout/stderr ignored. Resolves false on non-zero\n// exit, rejects only on abort or spawn failure (ENOENT).\nexport function gitOk (\n    repo:string, args:readonly string[], opts?:GitRunOpts\n):Promise<boolean>\n\n// Streaming: spawn('git', ...) with { signal }, stdout piped through a\n// SectionCapper chunk by chunk; stderr buffered.\nexport function gitPatch (\n    repo:string, args:readonly string[], maxSection:number,\n    opts?:GitRunOpts\n):Promise<{ out:Buffer, cut:string[] }>\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- `maxBuffer` must be effectively unlimited for buffered calls (Go has no\n  cap; `ls-files`/`ls-tree` on large repos exceed Node's 1 MiB default).\n  Patch output always goes through `gitPatch`, never `gitOut`.\n- `gitOk` replaces Go's `git(...)` helper (`rev-parse --verify --quiet`,\n  `merge-base` probe).\n- Abort: on `signal` abort, the child is killed and the promise rejects\n  with the signal's reason (an `AbortError`). Every best-effort `catch` in\n  this area (linguist, compactRenames, untracked read, repo head line,\n  temp dir cleanup) must rethrow when `signal?.aborted` is true, so\n  cancellation is never mistaken for \"no excludes\" or \"no renames\".\n- Every `diff` argument list is built with `DIFF_CONFIG` first (2.1).\n- `check-attr` stdin uses `input`; the extra env for it is\n  `{ GIT_ATTR_NOSYSTEM: '1', ...pinnedEnv }` and the args start with\n  `-c core.attributesFile=/dev/null`.\n- Temp index dir: `fs.mkdtemp(path.join(os.tmpdir(),\n  'roast-scope-idx-'))`, removed with `fs.rm(dir, { recursive: true,\n  force: true })` in `finally`.\n- Debug: namespace `roast:scope` may log each git argv and exit code;\n  never stdout content, file contents, or environment values.\n\n### 3.4 Bytes vs strings\n\nGo strings are byte strings; JS strings are UTF-16. To keep every size\nrule byte-exact:\n\n- `SectionCapper` works on `Buffer` chunks, finds `\\n` and the\n  `diff --git ` prefix on bytes, counts `used` in bytes, and collects\n  kept slices; `finish()` returns one `Buffer` decoded once with UTF-8\n  (`buf.toString('utf8')`, lossy) only for `Scope.diff`. `gitPatch`\n  returns the capped `Buffer`, and `diffBytes` keeps those exact bytes for\n  hashing and recording. A section cut mid-sequence therefore ends in\n  U+FFFD in `diff`, before the marker newline, matching what the Go bytes\n  become once JSON-encoded for a provider.\n- `compactRenames` and `splitSections` operate on `Buffer` (searching the\n  byte pattern `\\ndiff --git `); only a section's header and `rename`\n  lines are decoded for `classify` / `renameOf`.\n- `synthesizeNewFileDiff` reads into a `Buffer` (`fs.open` +\n  `read` up to `maxSection + 1`), splits on byte `0x0a`, measures header,\n  body and line lengths in bytes (header via `Buffer.byteLength`), and\n  decodes the final block once.\n- `fit` measures `diffBytes.length`, truncates `diffBytes` on a byte\n  offset (`subarray(0, maxBytes)` plus the marker bytes), and decodes the\n  result for `diff`. `fileDiffBytes` returns byte lengths.\n- Git name output is read as `Buffer` and NUL-split; `decodePaths(buf)`\n  returns `{ paths:string[], skipped:number }` using a fatal UTF-8\n  decoder, so non-UTF-8 names are skipped with a warning note (2.15).\n- `compareBytes(a, b)` = code-point order (e.g. compare\n  `Buffer.from(a)` with `Buffer.compare`); used for every sort.\n\n### 3.5 Survey helpers (repo scope)\n\n- `manifestQuote(name:string):{ text:string, quoted:boolean }` with\n  printable test `/^[\\p{L}\\p{M}\\p{N}\\p{P}\\p{S} ]$/u` per code point.\n  Names are already valid UTF-8 (`decodePaths`), so it takes a string.\n- There is no `cUnquote`: it served only branch scope, and `-z` removes\n  C-quoted name lists.\n\n## 4. Deliberate changes\n\n- Cancellation: `build` and `gitRoot` take an `AbortSignal` (Go's scope\n  package had no context). Aborting kills the running git child and\n  rejects with an `AbortError`; best-effort steps do not swallow it.\n- Windows is unsupported, so `/dev/null` is used literally for\n  `core.attributesFile` (Go used `os.DevNull`).\n- `fit` returns a new Scope instead of mutating in place (no observable\n  difference: the CLI already used the pre-fit diff for grounding).\n- Branch survey scope is not ported (deliberate change 23): no `branch`\n  scope, `includeWorktree`, `baseOID`, `mergeBaseOID`, `cUnquote`, or\n  branch tests.\n- Name lists and `ls-files --others` use `-z` (deliberate change 15).\n- Names that are not valid UTF-8 are skipped with a warning note\n  (deliberate change 24); git output is read as `Buffer`, and `Scope`\n  carries `diffBytes` so hashed or recorded diff bytes are exact.\n- The `fit` breakdown uses a stable sort (deliberate change 18).\n- Staged linguist exclusion lists a rename's deleted side too, via\n  `--no-renames` (deliberate change 16).\n- `diff.orderFile` is overridden on every `diff` invocation with\n  `DIFF_CONFIG` (deliberate change 17); verify the empty value against\n  git during implementation.\n- Go `unicode.IsPrint` vs JS `\\p{...}` differences for newly assigned\n  code points in `manifestQuote` are accepted.\n\nNothing else changes: other argument lists, note strings, manifest layout, and\nerror messages are as in Go.\n\n## 5. Test plan\n\nSeam: scope tests call `build()` (and `fit`, `gitRoot`) against real\ntemporary Git repositories created with `git init` plus\n`user.email`/`user.name` config, because `Notes`, `Manifest`, OIDs and\ncut lists are only partly observable through the CLI. Assertions use\nstructure (file lists, presence of a path in a diff header, counts of\n`SECTION_OMITTED_MARKER` occurrences, `+`/`-` line counts), not note\nwording; a note \"names a file\" check is a path substring check. A few\nCLI-level tests (in-process `run`, fake inference server, approved\nconfig) pin the flag wiring by asserting on `meta.json` `scope.files`\nand on the diff text the fake server received.\n\nTests that set `GIT_INDEX_FILE` or other git env must restore it.\nEvery test repo lives in a temp dir; no test runs git in this checkout.\n\n**Pathspecs and excludes**\n\n- Excludes filter both the diff text and `files`\n  (TestExcludesFilterBothDiffAndFiles).\n- Untracked synthesis honors `--paths` and excludes\n  (TestUntrackedSynthesisHonorsExcludesAndPaths).\n- Glob matcher cases from 2.14 (TestGlobMatch) -- pure function test.\n- Default scope with `scope` unset equals explicit `diff`\n  (TestDiffScopeUnchangedByDefaultAndExplicit).\n- Unknown scope string (including `branch`) is an error\n  (TestBuildUnknownScopeErrors).\n- A path with non-ASCII characters appears unquoted in `files` for tracked\n  and untracked changes, and the untracked file is synthesized (new,\n  replaces the Go quoted-path skip).\n- A file name that is not valid UTF-8 is skipped with a warning note and\n  absent from `files`; the build still succeeds. Create the name with a\n  `Buffer` path; skip the test on filesystems that refuse such names\n  (macOS APFS).\n\n**Renames**\n\n- A move is a delete+add under `--no-renames`, with excludes applied to\n  each side (TestRenameBecomesDeleteAdd).\n- Moved files with small edits collapse to rename sections with only the\n  edited lines (TestMovedFilesCollapseToRenameSections).\n- A pure move has a rename header and no hunks (TestPureMoveHasNoHunks).\n- A move across an exclude boundary is not paired; the excluded side\n  never appears (TestMovesAcrossExcludesAreNotPaired).\n- An uncommitted (staged) move collapses too (TestUncommittedMoveCollapses).\n- `fit` breakdown counts a collapsed move for both paths\n  (TestFitBreakdownCountsCollapsedMoves).\n- Record walk does not misread a path starting with `R` as a status\n  (TestMoveCollapsesAfterPathStartingWithR).\n\n**Staged mode**\n\n- Staged reads only the index; worktree-only edits are absent\n  (TestStagedModeReadsIndexOnly).\n- An alternate `GIT_INDEX_FILE` (as in `git commit <paths>`) is honored\n  and the shared index's staged content does not leak\n  (TestStagedModeHonorsGitIndexFile).\n\n**Base and empty repo**\n\n- No commits: diff against the empty tree, staged + untracked reviewed,\n  note present (TestEmptyRepoEmptyTreeFallback).\n- Base fallback notes: `using base` when not `origin/main`, `no usable\n  base` when none resolves (TestBaseFallbackNotes).\n\n**Size caps**\n\n- Two 24 MB files (one committed, one untracked) with a 64 KiB cap: diff\n  stays under 4x the cap, two omission markers, a small later file is\n  intact, notes name both cut files, all three in `files`\n  (TestBuildBoundsHugeFileDiffs). The Go allocated-bytes\n  assertion becomes an RSS bound: peak `process.memoryUsage().rss` growth\n  stays well below the file size (spec.md, Testing Decisions).\n- Capper keeps small sections byte-identical, cuts only the large one,\n  reports its name, and places the marker on its own line; with a huge\n  cap output equals input (TestCapSectionsKeepsSmallSectionsWhole) --\n  pure `SectionCapper` test.\n- `fit` truncation and descending breakdown\n  (TestFitTruncationAndBreakdown); equal-size files keep `files` order\n  (new, deliberate change 18).\n\n**Hardening**\n\n- A committed `.gitattributes` routing `*.go` to a named diff driver,\n  plus hostile global Git config defining that driver's textconv and an\n  external diff (each writing a marker file) and `diff.noprefix` /\n  `diff.mnemonicPrefix`, never runs either driver, for both\n  `base` and `uncommittedOnly` builds (TestBuildIgnoresUserDiffDrivers).\n  Isolate global config via `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` or `HOME`.\n- A global `diff.orderFile` that reverses the order does not change\n  section order, for staged, committed, and uncommitted builds (new,\n  deliberate change 17; also proves the empty-value override works).\n- Untracked secret-denylisted files are skipped and noted\n  (TestBuildSkipsDenylistedUntrackedFiles).\n- Untracked symlinks are not synthesized\n  (TestUntrackedSymlinkNotSynthesized).\n- Repo manifest quotes hostile names (newline, `<`, `>`) and adds the\n  explanation line (TestSurveyManifestQuotesHostileNames).\n\n**Linguist**\n\n- Marked paths are found; `=false` un-marks (TestLinguistIgnores).\n- No `.gitattributes` -> no excludes (TestLinguistIgnoresNoAttributes).\n- Generated files are excluded from diff and files\n  (TestLinguistGeneratedExcludedFromDiff).\n- A file named with glob metacharacters excludes only itself\n  (TestLinguistLiteralPathspec).\n- Staged mode reads attributes from the index, ignoring an unstaged\n  `.gitattributes` edit (TestLinguistStagedUsesIndexAttributes).\n- Exclusions honor the pinned tree, not a later-staged `.gitattributes`\n  (TestLinguistExclusionsHonorThePinnedTree).\n- A staged deletion of a generated file is excluded\n  (TestLinguistStagedDeletionExcluded).\n- A staged rename of a marked file excludes the old path too (new,\n  deliberate change 16).\n- A global `core.attributesFile` marking `*` cannot blank a review\n  (TestLinguistIgnoresGlobalAttributes); isolate `HOME` /\n  `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` / `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` in this test.\n- Untracked marked files are excluded\n  (TestLinguistGeneratedExcludesUntracked).\n\n**Repo survey**\n\n- Manifest lists tracked files, directory counts, HEAD line\n  (TestSurveyRepoManifest).\n- Excludes apply to the listing (TestSurveyRepoExcludesApplied).\n- No tracked files -> `isEmpty` (TestSurveyRepoEmptyWhenNoTrackedFiles).\n- Files reflect `headOID`, not the index\n  (TestSurveyRepoFilesReflectHeadOIDNotIndex).\n- Dirty tree drops linguist excludes rather than drifting\n  (TestSurveyRepoDirtyTreeDoesNotDriftLinguistExcludes).\n- A bare directory `--paths` value selects its subtree\n  (TestSurveyRepoDirectoryPathspecMatchesSubtree).\n\n**New in TS**\n\n- Aborting the signal during a large `gitPatch` kills git and rejects\n  with an `AbortError`, also when abort lands inside a best-effort step.\n- `diffBytes` equals the bytes git emitted for sections under the cap\n  (byte-exact for non-UTF-8 content), while `diff` decodes with U+FFFD.\n- Sorting uses code-point order (a path with an astral character sorts\n  after one with U+FFFD).\n\n## 6. Go baseline references\n\nAt commit `cd08f10`:\n\n- `internal/scope/scope.go` -- Build, survey scopes (branch not ported),\n  linguist, globs,\n  untracked synthesis, Fit, GitRoot.\n- `internal/scope/patch.go` -- capSections, sectionName, truncationNote,\n  DefaultMaxFileDiffBytes, sectionOmittedMarker.\n- `internal/scope/renames.go` -- compactRenames, splitSections, classify,\n  renameOf.\n- `internal/scope/scope_test.go`, `linguist_test.go`,\n  `hardening_test.go`.\n- `internal/cli/cli.go` (flag definitions, `--scope` validation,\n  BuildOpts wiring, Fit call, run-record scope meta).\n- `internal/context/tree.go` (`PinIndexTree`).\n- `internal/snapshot/snapshot.go` (`DefaultDenylist`, `Denylisted`).\n\n## 7. Resolved\n\nOpen questions from the draft, resolved by the maintainer (spec.md,\n\"Deliberate changes from the Go baseline\"):\n\n1. Branch survey scope is not ported (deliberate change 23).\n2. Diff name lists and `ls-files --others` use `-z` (deliberate change 15).\n3. Non-UTF-8 file names are skipped with a warning (deliberate change 24);\n   git output is read as `Buffer` (cross-cutting \"Bytes and text\").\n4. The fit breakdown uses a stable sort (deliberate change 18).\n5. Staged generated-file exclusion lists renames' deleted side too\n   (deliberate change 16).\n6. The user's `diff.orderFile` is overridden (deliberate change 17);\n   verify the mechanism against git during implementation.\n7. The huge-file test asserts an RSS bound (spec.md, Testing Decisions).\n8. Go/Node Unicode printable differences are accepted.\n","numLines":972,"startLine":1,"totalLines":972}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GYYSDjzVRHZzsux3R9LGaS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
